WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.567 --> 00:00:07.233
Today, though, we are delighted to

00:00:07.697 --> 00:00:09.500
welcome Hannah Pollin-Galay,

00:00:10.707 --> 00:00:12.428
who is a senior lecturer of Yiddish and

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:13.756
Holocaust Studies in the Department of

00:00:13.757 --> 00:00:15.527
Literature at Tel Aviv University,

00:00:16.217 --> 00:00:18.556
and the author of a book that

00:00:19.337 --> 00:00:21.502
is right here, Ecologies,

00:00:23.477 --> 00:00:25.426
Ecologies of Witnessing: Language, Place

00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:27.136
and Holocaust Testimony, which came out on

00:00:27.137 --> 00:00:29.206
Yale University, in Yale University Press

00:00:29.297 --> 00:00:30.308
in 2018.

00:00:31.187 --> 00:00:33.046
And we have some information about the

00:00:33.047 --> 00:00:34.867
book outside.

00:00:34.877 --> 00:00:37.277
So grab a flier on your way out.

00:00:38.687 --> 00:00:41.477
And Hannah is

00:00:41.627 --> 00:00:43.966
a remarkable polyglot scholar

00:00:43.997 --> 00:00:46.276
with a broad intellectual range,

00:00:47.207 --> 00:00:49.876
writing articles on everything from

00:00:50.027 --> 00:00:52.876
Avrom Sutzkever's poetry, to Soviet

00:00:53.027 --> 00:00:54.399
songs, and even contemporary

00:00:55.847 --> 00:00:56.847
Israeli politics.

00:00:57.227 --> 00:00:59.317
For instance, for the Nation and

00:00:59.357 --> 00:01:01.486
other venues. And she is a, as a

00:01:01.487 --> 00:01:03.447
representative of an organization called

00:01:03.677 --> 00:01:04.756
Standing Together.

00:01:05.357 --> 00:01:06.976
So she's a scholar who does not just

00:01:06.977 --> 00:01:09.436
engage with her field of study solely

00:01:09.437 --> 00:01:11.866
for the purpose of scholarly production.

00:01:12.287 --> 00:01:14.356
But I think she clearly draws from her

00:01:14.357 --> 00:01:16.396
work to inform her fight for social

00:01:16.397 --> 00:01:17.706
justice causes today.

00:01:19.427 --> 00:01:21.256
She'll be speaking about her book.

00:01:21.407 --> 00:01:23.917
And once she has talked about 20,

00:01:23.927 --> 00:01:26.296
25 minutes and

00:01:26.507 --> 00:01:28.636
afterwards, her presentation will

00:01:28.637 --> 00:01:29.857
be followed by a response from Deborah

00:01:30.197 --> 00:01:31.961
Dwork, who was the founding director

00:01:32.357 --> 00:01:34.246
of the Strassler Center for Holocaust and

00:01:34.247 --> 00:01:36.556
Genocide Studies and is the Rose Professor

00:01:36.557 --> 00:01:37.996
of History at Clark University.

00:01:38.477 --> 00:01:40.486
She's written numerous articles, chapters

00:01:40.547 --> 00:01:41.896
and many books. I'm just going to name a

00:01:41.897 --> 00:01:42.897
few.

00:01:43.217 --> 00:01:46.006
Most recently, 2011 on Northwestern

00:01:46.007 --> 00:01:48.065
University Press was A Boy in Terezin: The

00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:49.237
Private Diary of Pavel Weiner

00:01:50.057 --> 00:01:51.256
introductions and invitations.

00:01:52.607 --> 00:01:54.518
She's coauthored a number of books with

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.497
Robert Van Pelt, Flight from the Reich,

00:01:56.877 --> 00:01:58.817
1933 to 1946,

00:01:59.197 --> 00:02:01.607
that came out 2009, and Auschwitz:

00:02:03.617 --> 00:02:04.727
1270 to the Present.

00:02:04.997 --> 00:02:06.616
And the German edition of that book, I

00:02:06.617 --> 00:02:08.836
would note, was voted the

00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:10.486
number one title in the German National

00:02:10.487 --> 00:02:12.857
Book Critics List in November

00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:14.297
1998, and

00:02:15.377 --> 00:02:17.356
by Newsweek, it was voted as one of the 10

00:02:17.357 --> 00:02:19.126
best books about Poland during World War

00:02:19.127 --> 00:02:21.276
II, in August 2019-- 20,

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:22.407
2009.

00:02:23.297 --> 00:02:24.992
There was an Emmy Award nominated

00:02:25.337 --> 00:02:27.227
documented based on this work

00:02:27.617 --> 00:02:29.026
called Auschwitz, the Blueprints of

00:02:29.027 --> 00:02:31.246
Genocide produced by the BBC,

00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:33.541
and it aired in the UK and

00:02:34.427 --> 00:02:35.427
in the US.

00:02:35.797 --> 00:02:37.561
And it also served as a as a central

00:02:37.577 --> 00:02:39.316
source for a seven part documentary

00:02:39.317 --> 00:02:41.447
series, also produced by the BBC called

00:02:41.537 --> 00:02:43.187
Auschwitz: Inside the Nazi State.

00:02:44.357 --> 00:02:46.268
The books received National Jewish Book

00:02:46.337 --> 00:02:48.496
Awards and many other awards.

00:02:48.527 --> 00:02:50.776
But nonetheless, we're very happy to have

00:02:51.317 --> 00:02:52.317
you here, Deborah.

00:02:52.577 --> 00:02:54.376
And with that, let's welcome

00:02:54.827 --> 00:02:56.395
Hannah to the podium to give her

00:02:56.927 --> 00:02:57.927
presentation.

00:03:14.817 --> 00:03:16.436
Hi, everyone. Thank you so much for

00:03:16.437 --> 00:03:18.327
coming. Thank you, Stephen, for that

00:03:18.477 --> 00:03:19.886
generous introduction.

00:03:20.007 --> 00:03:22.136
It's really quite moving to talk

00:03:22.137 --> 00:03:24.867
about this book here at the Fortunoff

00:03:24.917 --> 00:03:26.547
Archive, since I did

00:03:27.057 --> 00:03:29.126
part of my research here and then was

00:03:29.487 --> 00:03:31.526
generously assisted by Joanne Rudof,

00:03:31.767 --> 00:03:33.727
who's here, and she stayed in touch with

00:03:33.927 --> 00:03:36.017
me for years afterwards, to, to

00:03:36.177 --> 00:03:38.606
really help me see whatever

00:03:38.637 --> 00:03:40.646
I wanted to see about the archive.

00:03:40.677 --> 00:03:42.539
And I think that as I, and I did write

00:03:43.017 --> 00:03:44.389
this in the acknowledgments,

00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:48.026
that that's, that's not a technicality.

00:03:48.417 --> 00:03:50.876
Helping researchers in the humanities

00:03:50.967 --> 00:03:53.577
in that way is it is a statement.

00:03:53.607 --> 00:03:55.273
It's a vast statement of values to

00:03:55.827 --> 00:03:56.827
be both someone who

00:03:58.047 --> 00:03:59.762
aggregates and organizes testimony,

00:04:00.207 --> 00:04:02.547
as well as someone who facilitates

00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:04.186
open research and new questions

00:04:04.857 --> 00:04:06.567
on it. So I really thank you for that and

00:04:06.927 --> 00:04:08.246
thank you for having me here.

00:04:09.147 --> 00:04:10.470
I want to start by, I'm not

00:04:11.307 --> 00:04:13.196
going to talk about the Israeli side of

00:04:13.197 --> 00:04:14.516
the book because at the time limit.

00:04:14.547 --> 00:04:15.776
But if you want to ask me about that

00:04:15.777 --> 00:04:17.906
later, I brought the book so I can look

00:04:17.907 --> 00:04:18.907
things up.

00:04:21.357 --> 00:04:23.817
So I want to start with that

00:04:23.937 --> 00:04:26.186
historic date that Stephen just

00:04:26.187 --> 00:04:28.616
mentioned, May 2nd,

00:04:28.947 --> 00:04:31.047
May 2, 1979.

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:33.097
And I do believe that that's an historic

00:04:33.627 --> 00:04:34.627
date.

00:04:35.037 --> 00:04:37.556
And that recording of Holocaust

00:04:37.557 --> 00:04:40.226
testimony in the last two decades

00:04:40.227 --> 00:04:42.537
of the 20th century should be

00:04:42.567 --> 00:04:43.988
considered a historical event

00:04:44.847 --> 00:04:46.976
in its own right, an event

00:04:46.977 --> 00:04:48.741
of current commemoration of archival

00:04:49.347 --> 00:04:50.817
creation, a public declaration

00:04:52.047 --> 00:04:54.447
of values, and also critically

00:04:54.867 --> 00:04:56.607
an events of translation.

00:04:57.567 --> 00:04:59.396
Scholars have rightly asserted that the

00:04:59.397 --> 00:05:01.856
scale and technological practices

00:05:01.857 --> 00:05:03.278
of recent Holocaust testimony

00:05:04.047 --> 00:05:06.236
projects have changed the scope

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:08.667
and circulation of witness narrative.

00:05:09.357 --> 00:05:12.116
But what remains unaddressed

00:05:12.447 --> 00:05:14.396
is how these same testimonies taking

00:05:14.397 --> 00:05:15.622
projects also attempted a

00:05:16.557 --> 00:05:19.016
radical act of translation,

00:05:19.377 --> 00:05:21.776
a transference of knowledge and ideas

00:05:21.837 --> 00:05:24.656
across lingual and geographic borders.

00:05:25.317 --> 00:05:27.447
For example, the USC Shoah

00:05:27.567 --> 00:05:29.486
Foundation Institute alone

00:05:29.907 --> 00:05:31.279
has recorded testimony in 52

00:05:32.067 --> 00:05:34.376
different countries and in 36 different

00:05:34.377 --> 00:05:36.626
languages, bringing with it

00:05:36.627 --> 00:05:39.026
potentially specific

00:05:39.477 --> 00:05:41.906
American rooted concepts of history,

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:43.256
selfhood and suffering.

00:05:44.217 --> 00:05:46.226
My research investigates what happened and

00:05:46.227 --> 00:05:48.656
what happens to the testimonial enterprise

00:05:48.987 --> 00:05:51.026
and the conceptual web on which it rests

00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:53.178
when it arrives in different geographic

00:05:53.487 --> 00:05:55.476
and linguistic contexts.

00:05:55.527 --> 00:05:57.867
Or, as I term them, ecologies

00:05:57.897 --> 00:05:58.897
of witnessing.

00:05:59.487 --> 00:06:01.349
Viewed comparatively, victim testimony

00:06:01.677 --> 00:06:03.490
footage reveals profound distinctions

00:06:04.377 --> 00:06:06.435
in how people from different settings tell

00:06:06.747 --> 00:06:08.756
the truth about the violence that they

00:06:08.757 --> 00:06:10.167
endured in the Holocaust.

00:06:10.717 --> 00:06:13.557
So I approach this question of translation

00:06:13.917 --> 00:06:16.317
through a specific case study.

00:06:17.697 --> 00:06:20.531
I look into Holocaust testimonies,

00:06:21.597 --> 00:06:24.387
video and audio Holocaust testimonies

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:27.297
of Lithuanian Jews who were all born

00:06:27.747 --> 00:06:29.667
before 1935

00:06:30.147 --> 00:06:31.813
and they survived the Holocaust in

00:06:32.307 --> 00:06:33.386
one way or another.

00:06:34.257 --> 00:06:35.966
So they have that much in common.

00:06:36.027 --> 00:06:38.187
And then after the Holocaust, they

00:06:38.247 --> 00:06:40.167
either stayed in Lithuania,

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:43.287
moved to Israel or moved to the U.S.

00:06:43.377 --> 00:06:45.837
and they chose to give their testimony

00:06:46.377 --> 00:06:47.602
in this later period from

00:06:48.717 --> 00:06:51.087
1979 on, in

00:06:51.147 --> 00:06:53.096
one of three languages, in Yiddish,

00:06:53.127 --> 00:06:54.567
English or in Hebrew.

00:06:56.577 --> 00:06:58.856
So why did

00:06:58.857 --> 00:07:01.346
I choose Lithuania for this

00:07:01.737 --> 00:07:02.726
case study?

00:07:02.727 --> 00:07:04.677
I hope that this map will just give us

00:07:05.127 --> 00:07:07.406
a reminder of how

00:07:07.917 --> 00:07:09.681
Lithuania was originally annexed the

00:07:09.987 --> 00:07:11.966
Soviet Union in 1940

00:07:12.477 --> 00:07:15.086
and then reincorporated into the USSR

00:07:15.117 --> 00:07:16.262
after World War II.

00:07:17.397 --> 00:07:19.317
So the reincorporation

00:07:19.587 --> 00:07:22.346
meant that apart from those Lithuanian

00:07:22.347 --> 00:07:24.307
Jews who happened to have pre-war pulled

00:07:24.537 --> 00:07:27.026
their citizenship, Holocaust survivors

00:07:27.027 --> 00:07:29.156
who returned to Lithuania after the war

00:07:29.157 --> 00:07:30.927
were required to stay there

00:07:31.347 --> 00:07:33.466
at least until the 1970s.

00:07:33.807 --> 00:07:35.757
So that meant that there was actually

00:07:36.267 --> 00:07:38.966
a somewhat robust community

00:07:39.017 --> 00:07:41.486
of, of Holocaust survivors

00:07:41.487 --> 00:07:43.376
in Lithuania after the war.

00:07:46.107 --> 00:07:48.526
The community hovered around 30,000

00:07:48.597 --> 00:07:49.597
members

00:07:51.207 --> 00:07:53.847
and Yiddish was actually quite prominent

00:07:53.967 --> 00:07:56.366
in their communal interactions

00:07:56.367 --> 00:07:57.597
for different reasons.

00:07:58.407 --> 00:08:00.417
So that

00:08:00.657 --> 00:08:02.967
ecology interests me

00:08:02.997 --> 00:08:05.427
greatly. And at the same time, of course,

00:08:06.747 --> 00:08:07.825
most of the Lithuanian

00:08:09.177 --> 00:08:11.027
Jewish survivors moved,

00:08:11.787 --> 00:08:14.306
somehow managed to move to either

00:08:15.237 --> 00:08:17.050
the U.S., or North America, or Israel

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:19.226
after the war, and they were very

00:08:19.227 --> 00:08:21.138
instrumental in those places in leading

00:08:21.537 --> 00:08:23.216
the way in indifferent commemoration

00:08:23.217 --> 00:08:24.217
efforts.

00:08:24.297 --> 00:08:25.947
So for those reasons, I thought that

00:08:25.977 --> 00:08:28.046
Lithuanian Jewry would make an especially

00:08:28.047 --> 00:08:29.337
interesting case study.

00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:33.566
For roughly a decade, I dwelt with over

00:08:33.567 --> 00:08:35.233
100 testimonies of Lithuanian Jews

00:08:35.967 --> 00:08:38.096
from these three settings, both

00:08:38.097 --> 00:08:40.496
as an interviewer and as a listener.

00:08:41.787 --> 00:08:43.796
Over the course of that process, I became

00:08:44.037 --> 00:08:46.106
convinced that the witnesses language

00:08:46.107 --> 00:08:47.283
place environment deeply

00:08:48.237 --> 00:08:50.547
informs her mode of truth telling.

00:08:51.417 --> 00:08:53.132
The witness's ecological resources,

00:08:53.637 --> 00:08:55.303
the words, images and social norms

00:08:56.037 --> 00:08:57.997
at her disposal shaped critical concepts

00:08:58.797 --> 00:09:01.587
like justice, guilt, strength,

00:09:01.647 --> 00:09:03.264
pain, and even love and family in

00:09:03.807 --> 00:09:04.807
the Holocaust.

00:09:05.517 --> 00:09:07.557
It is to this last point of difference,

00:09:07.587 --> 00:09:09.647
ideas about family and belonging in

00:09:09.687 --> 00:09:11.451
Holocaust narratives on which I will

00:09:11.877 --> 00:09:13.377
focus my lecture today.

00:09:16.047 --> 00:09:18.477
A lot of the most prominent Holocaust

00:09:18.507 --> 00:09:21.026
narratives in American culture

00:09:21.027 --> 00:09:22.917
are actually family stories.

00:09:24.297 --> 00:09:25.797
The Diary of Anne Frank.

00:09:25.857 --> 00:09:27.927
One of the reasons why it's,

00:09:29.487 --> 00:09:31.527
why it's considered appropriate for

00:09:31.676 --> 00:09:33.356
seventh graders is that it tells a

00:09:33.357 --> 00:09:34.435
family's story and the

00:09:35.487 --> 00:09:37.256
family's story accompanies the Holocaust

00:09:37.257 --> 00:09:38.257
story.

00:09:39.327 --> 00:09:41.071
Likewise, Art Spiegelman's Maus

00:09:44.037 --> 00:09:46.406
narrates the Holocaust through the lens

00:09:46.407 --> 00:09:48.476
of an intimate father-son relationship.

00:09:49.206 --> 00:09:50.823
And at the entrance to the United

00:09:51.507 --> 00:09:53.637
States Holocaust Memorial Museum,

00:09:54.567 --> 00:09:56.966
there is a small free exhibit

00:09:56.967 --> 00:09:59.157
of sorts called Daniel's Story,

00:10:00.447 --> 00:10:02.906
which physicalizes that family

00:10:02.907 --> 00:10:05.576
focus that we see in other literary

00:10:05.577 --> 00:10:06.577
texts.

00:10:06.837 --> 00:10:07.837
And I think that

00:10:09.447 --> 00:10:10.447
the family framework

00:10:11.757 --> 00:10:13.916
here, that the nuclear family,

00:10:13.917 --> 00:10:15.093
as it's depicted through

00:10:16.167 --> 00:10:18.446
a small household and the intimate

00:10:18.447 --> 00:10:20.113
spaces of a bedroom, a bathroom, a

00:10:20.517 --> 00:10:23.127
kitchen, is deemed appropriate

00:10:23.217 --> 00:10:25.946
as an entrance space, an architectural

00:10:25.947 --> 00:10:27.172
choice, because family is

00:10:28.137 --> 00:10:30.806
thought to be a universal and inviting

00:10:30.807 --> 00:10:33.656
topic and much easier, supposedly,

00:10:33.657 --> 00:10:35.906
than politics or,

00:10:36.657 --> 00:10:39.477
or, or, culture, or resistance.

00:10:40.167 --> 00:10:42.656
And I would like to differ

00:10:42.657 --> 00:10:44.306
with that, from that position.

00:10:44.337 --> 00:10:46.107
I think that that family

00:10:46.647 --> 00:10:48.116
is not universal

00:10:48.867 --> 00:10:50.936
and not in Holocaust testimonies, and that

00:10:50.937 --> 00:10:52.799
the differences between family stories

00:10:53.307 --> 00:10:54.728
across lingual and geographic

00:10:55.857 --> 00:10:58.386
boundaries are quite significant

00:10:59.027 --> 00:11:00.987
to how they see the Holocaust as a whole

00:11:01.227 --> 00:11:03.267
and to how witnesses see the act

00:11:03.417 --> 00:11:04.497
of testimony.

00:11:05.457 --> 00:11:07.417
So in this lecture, I'm going to examine

00:11:07.527 --> 00:11:09.956
just one rich testimony

00:11:09.957 --> 00:11:12.386
from two of the ecologies in question,

00:11:12.837 --> 00:11:14.816
an English speaker from North America and

00:11:14.817 --> 00:11:16.467
a Yiddish speaker from Lithuania.

00:11:17.547 --> 00:11:20.547
This isn't quantitative sociology.

00:11:20.577 --> 00:11:22.341
So the examples that I read, the way

00:11:22.827 --> 00:11:24.542
that I work with examples is not by

00:11:24.597 --> 00:11:26.966
finding a median or mean

00:11:26.967 --> 00:11:28.584
example and claiming that they're

00:11:28.587 --> 00:11:29.861
representative of everyone

00:11:30.987 --> 00:11:31.987
else like them.

00:11:32.337 --> 00:11:35.156
But rather, I look for examples

00:11:36.147 --> 00:11:38.336
from the group that I studied who pick

00:11:38.337 --> 00:11:40.886
up on important

00:11:40.887 --> 00:11:43.027
pressure points in rich ways.

00:11:43.177 --> 00:11:44.177
OK.

00:11:45.807 --> 00:11:47.936
So the first

00:11:48.447 --> 00:11:50.366
testimony that I'll discuss, it's a, it's

00:11:50.367 --> 00:11:52.227
a Fortunoff testimony

00:11:52.677 --> 00:11:54.716
from a woman named Suzanne H.

00:11:56.517 --> 00:11:58.644
From the start of Suzanne's testimony,

00:11:58.827 --> 00:12:00.896
her parents are not only themes of

00:12:00.897 --> 00:12:01.946
veneration.

00:12:02.487 --> 00:12:04.286
They are the filter through which she

00:12:04.287 --> 00:12:06.716
retrieves history, helping

00:12:06.717 --> 00:12:08.236
her to discern the relevance of

00:12:08.757 --> 00:12:11.007
information and images that surface

00:12:11.247 --> 00:12:12.507
from pre-war life.

00:12:13.227 --> 00:12:15.236
For example, Suzanne recalls the physical

00:12:15.537 --> 00:12:16.860
space of her childhood as a

00:12:17.667 --> 00:12:20.186
reflection of her parents' personalities.

00:12:20.847 --> 00:12:23.066
Their home in the suburbs of Vilna

00:12:23.127 --> 00:12:24.695
was, and I quote, "a very pretty

00:12:25.497 --> 00:12:27.376
building with a garden in front.

00:12:27.867 --> 00:12:29.966
Every spring my father used to plant

00:12:29.967 --> 00:12:31.017
flowers in front.

00:12:31.437 --> 00:12:33.250
I used to look forward to it," taking

00:12:33.987 --> 00:12:36.117
us on a tour through her

00:12:36.177 --> 00:12:38.486
house and in a way through

00:12:38.487 --> 00:12:40.826
her parental-child relationships.

00:12:40.917 --> 00:12:43.166
She then relates, "I remember my mother

00:12:43.167 --> 00:12:45.146
having very cheerful accessories."

00:12:45.897 --> 00:12:47.778
Like her immediate physical surroundings,

00:12:47.877 --> 00:12:49.543
the larger political atmosphere in

00:12:50.097 --> 00:12:52.646
Vilna also appears in relation

00:12:52.647 --> 00:12:54.477
to her parents' personalities.

00:12:55.497 --> 00:12:57.446
Suzanne tells that even though they were

00:12:57.447 --> 00:12:58.966
highly secular Jews, her father

00:12:59.877 --> 00:13:01.788
chose to send her to a Jewish school as

00:13:02.037 --> 00:13:03.409
a child rather than a Polish

00:13:04.287 --> 00:13:05.953
one, because, quote, "he was angry

00:13:06.807 --> 00:13:08.366
at the way that the Polish people looked

00:13:08.367 --> 00:13:11.367
at the Jews." So in this formulation,

00:13:11.397 --> 00:13:12.867
antisemitism becomes a reality

00:13:14.007 --> 00:13:16.196
of past experience, something

00:13:16.197 --> 00:13:18.537
to which she is a credible eyewitness

00:13:18.837 --> 00:13:21.267
when it impacts her father emotionally.

00:13:22.227 --> 00:13:23.991
Bringing his social perspective into

00:13:24.417 --> 00:13:26.426
the war years, Suzanne recounts events of

00:13:26.487 --> 00:13:28.153
the Holocaust through the prism of

00:13:28.737 --> 00:13:30.452
personal perception and interaction

00:13:30.957 --> 00:13:32.186
with her parents and brother.

00:13:33.177 --> 00:13:35.037
Early after the German invasion of Vilna

00:13:35.067 --> 00:13:37.196
on June 24, 1941,

00:13:37.467 --> 00:13:39.236
Suzanne recalls a conversation with her

00:13:39.237 --> 00:13:41.087
mother during one of the early roundups

00:13:41.097 --> 00:13:42.046
that year.

00:13:42.047 --> 00:13:44.007
"I kept her, I kept on telling mother,

00:13:44.037 --> 00:13:45.386
'Oh, don't worry, everything's gonna be

00:13:45.387 --> 00:13:47.248
okay,' even though I was petrified."

00:13:48.087 --> 00:13:49.312
This intimate dialogue is

00:13:50.307 --> 00:13:52.136
what makes the event real.

00:13:53.157 --> 00:13:54.823
Sparing in details about the scene

00:13:55.557 --> 00:13:58.106
around her, Suzanne is very precise

00:13:58.107 --> 00:13:59.786
about her and her mother's emotional

00:13:59.787 --> 00:14:00.787
exchange.

00:14:02.417 --> 00:14:04.230
Suzanne and her family survived these

00:14:04.617 --> 00:14:06.626
initial round ups and were forced to move

00:14:06.837 --> 00:14:08.336
into the Vilna ghetto at the beginning of

00:14:08.337 --> 00:14:09.337
September.

00:14:09.987 --> 00:14:11.576
After a short while, they were able to

00:14:11.577 --> 00:14:13.977
move into the Keilis fur factory

00:14:14.007 --> 00:14:16.237
on the outskirts of the city, which was

00:14:16.647 --> 00:14:18.666
one of two small work camps

00:14:19.257 --> 00:14:20.696
just outside of Vilna.

00:14:20.857 --> 00:14:21.857
Keilis and H.K.P.,

00:14:23.217 --> 00:14:24.442
each of which was a small

00:14:25.527 --> 00:14:27.438
brick enclosure with roughly a thousand

00:14:27.837 --> 00:14:29.997
Jews working and

00:14:30.087 --> 00:14:31.087
imprisoned there.

00:14:31.767 --> 00:14:33.596
Suzanne explains that, though, that since

00:14:33.597 --> 00:14:35.126
her family did not have an official

00:14:35.127 --> 00:14:37.366
license to be in Keilis,

00:14:37.677 --> 00:14:39.537
they were eventually forced to move back

00:14:39.897 --> 00:14:40.897
into the ghetto.

00:14:41.607 --> 00:14:43.322
She briefly summarizes the cultural

00:14:43.947 --> 00:14:45.907
milieu of the ghetto that she found when

00:14:46.077 --> 00:14:47.267
she returned there.

00:14:48.117 --> 00:14:49.136
They had a theater.

00:14:49.197 --> 00:14:50.307
They had some culture.

00:14:50.337 --> 00:14:51.536
They even opened a restaurant.

00:14:51.747 --> 00:14:52.747
And that's it.

00:14:52.767 --> 00:14:55.317
Events unfold independently

00:14:55.347 --> 00:14:56.783
from this cultural milieu.

00:14:57.807 --> 00:14:59.620
The main stage of causality is within

00:15:00.057 --> 00:15:01.946
the intimate emotional space of her

00:15:01.947 --> 00:15:04.376
family. We can also can, we can almost

00:15:04.377 --> 00:15:07.136
think of Suzanne's relationship

00:15:07.287 --> 00:15:09.567
in her memory narrative to

00:15:09.597 --> 00:15:11.847
her cultural milieu in the way

00:15:12.177 --> 00:15:14.547
that Erich Auerbach describes

00:15:14.877 --> 00:15:16.894
the protagonist's in

00:15:17.148 --> 00:15:18.148
Stendhal's work.

00:15:18.177 --> 00:15:19.843
He says, "a man seems to be thrown

00:15:20.877 --> 00:15:22.967
by chance into his milieu-- to the milieu

00:15:23.337 --> 00:15:24.507
in which he lives."

00:15:26.607 --> 00:15:27.607
Susanne's focus on

00:15:28.767 --> 00:15:31.136
her nuclear

00:15:31.137 --> 00:15:33.446
family relationship becomes even more

00:15:33.447 --> 00:15:35.260
striking as the narrative progresses.

00:15:35.337 --> 00:15:37.157
The family sneaks back into the Keilis

00:15:37.587 --> 00:15:39.446
work camp when the Vilna ghetto is about

00:15:39.447 --> 00:15:40.447
to be liquidated.

00:15:41.157 --> 00:15:42.970
Their, her parents are caught outside

00:15:43.527 --> 00:15:45.086
of the camp and it's, it's a bit

00:15:45.087 --> 00:15:46.396
complicated. I won't go into it.

00:15:46.407 --> 00:15:47.926
The guards agree to release her

00:15:48.567 --> 00:15:50.066
father, but not her mother.

00:15:50.727 --> 00:15:52.491
And Suzanne envisions a conversation

00:15:53.097 --> 00:15:54.716
between the two of them, her mother

00:15:54.717 --> 00:15:56.546
saying, "you have to stay and find the

00:15:56.547 --> 00:15:58.466
children. You have to raise the children,

00:15:58.657 --> 00:16:00.895
he gave her all the jewelry he had.

00:16:01.197 --> 00:16:02.197
That was it.

00:16:02.517 --> 00:16:04.196
Later, I heard tahat they made them take

00:16:04.197 --> 00:16:06.146
their clothes off at Ponary and shot

00:16:06.147 --> 00:16:07.147
them." The loss of

00:16:08.307 --> 00:16:10.436
Suzanne's mother, the quick depiction of

00:16:10.437 --> 00:16:12.069
her being stripped and shot at Ponary

00:16:12.867 --> 00:16:14.631
constitutes the turning point in her

00:16:14.997 --> 00:16:16.565
view of history, the moment when

00:16:17.277 --> 00:16:19.887
life changed from difficult to abnormal.

00:16:21.387 --> 00:16:23.347
Evidence of this catastrophic rift comes

00:16:23.637 --> 00:16:25.256
from within Suzanne's body.

00:16:26.007 --> 00:16:27.806
She says, "I was 12 years old and I used

00:16:27.807 --> 00:16:28.736
to wet my bed.

00:16:28.737 --> 00:16:30.386
I later learned that you want your mother

00:16:30.387 --> 00:16:31.797
back and this is what we did.

00:16:32.187 --> 00:16:34.196
I was so embarrassed at the time and my

00:16:34.227 --> 00:16:36.416
aunt used to tell-- to yell at me and

00:16:36.447 --> 00:16:38.007
I didn't know what to do."

00:16:38.667 --> 00:16:40.233
So she's focused on the preconscious,

00:16:40.947 --> 00:16:43.196
physiological reaction to loss,

00:16:43.797 --> 00:16:46.086
and in doing so she frames this

00:16:46.087 --> 00:16:48.517
moment as a break in the natural order

00:16:48.577 --> 00:16:49.577
of mind-body-health.

00:16:52.387 --> 00:16:54.906
Some people, like

00:16:54.907 --> 00:16:57.577
one of my very own dissertation advisers,

00:16:58.087 --> 00:16:59.087
David Roskies,

00:17:00.397 --> 00:17:01.671
have looked at testimonies

00:17:02.527 --> 00:17:04.716
like this in the Fortunoff Archive

00:17:05.287 --> 00:17:07.267
and criticized the

00:17:07.657 --> 00:17:10.387
interviewers or the institutional culture

00:17:10.837 --> 00:17:12.062
for supposedly distancing

00:17:12.997 --> 00:17:15.156
the survivors story, what, from what

00:17:15.157 --> 00:17:16.896
they consider history.

00:17:18.667 --> 00:17:21.306
And I disagree with that normative

00:17:21.307 --> 00:17:22.307
position.

00:17:23.077 --> 00:17:24.396
It's not,

00:17:25.897 --> 00:17:27.577
it's not out of ignorance or

00:17:27.597 --> 00:17:29.706
shortsightedness that witnesses

00:17:29.707 --> 00:17:31.977
like Suzanne downplay

00:17:31.997 --> 00:17:33.369
a distinction-- depiction of

00:17:34.117 --> 00:17:36.277
the larger milieu from which they came.

00:17:37.507 --> 00:17:39.369
In Dominick LaCapra's formulation, the

00:17:39.487 --> 00:17:41.767
story of a greater Jewish collective

00:17:41.797 --> 00:17:44.047
is absent rather than lost

00:17:44.077 --> 00:17:45.966
in testimonies like this.

00:17:46.507 --> 00:17:48.606
In Suzanne's framework and in the book, I

00:17:48.607 --> 00:17:50.047
do argue that there are

00:17:51.517 --> 00:17:53.686
a great number of other survivors in this

00:17:53.687 --> 00:17:55.353
ecology who share this perspective

00:17:56.047 --> 00:17:57.047
in one way or another.

00:17:57.367 --> 00:17:59.327
The Holocaust appears most damaging when

00:17:59.347 --> 00:18:00.572
it unhinges the emotional

00:18:01.627 --> 00:18:03.547
economy of each household.

00:18:03.967 --> 00:18:05.290
So if catastrophe occurs in

00:18:06.097 --> 00:18:07.861
the psychic and sensational realm of

00:18:08.287 --> 00:18:10.716
each family, then that is where history

00:18:10.717 --> 00:18:11.717
happens.

00:18:12.097 --> 00:18:14.057
That one had better examine the singular

00:18:14.437 --> 00:18:16.926
family's stories, one at a time,

00:18:16.927 --> 00:18:18.985
moving household to household, in order to

00:18:19.177 --> 00:18:20.856
assess the damage of history.

00:18:21.367 --> 00:18:23.557
It is simply not especially relevant

00:18:23.827 --> 00:18:25.542
to reconstruct social externalities

00:18:25.987 --> 00:18:27.905
of Jewish belonging when internalities

00:18:28.747 --> 00:18:30.757
appear as such powerful tools

00:18:31.027 --> 00:18:33.216
for telling and explaining the truth

00:18:33.217 --> 00:18:34.217
about the past.

00:18:35.437 --> 00:18:38.166
And in this sense, also, I distance myself

00:18:38.557 --> 00:18:39.557
from some of the,

00:18:40.667 --> 00:18:42.276
I guess you could call them more

00:18:42.277 --> 00:18:44.347
simplistic or aggressive

00:18:44.437 --> 00:18:46.487
critiques of of

00:18:46.657 --> 00:18:48.607
psychoanalysis that are out.

00:18:48.637 --> 00:18:50.497
We discussed some of them yesterday at

00:18:51.517 --> 00:18:54.067
the event for Carolyn Dean's book, where

00:18:54.987 --> 00:18:55.987
in denaturalizing

00:18:57.667 --> 00:18:59.235
personal experience is a mode of

00:18:59.527 --> 00:19:01.307
remembering history. And I do think it's

00:19:01.417 --> 00:19:03.516
worth denaturalizing and not considering

00:19:03.517 --> 00:19:04.517
it universal.

00:19:04.897 --> 00:19:06.777
People then delegitimize

00:19:07.027 --> 00:19:09.666
it. And that's just

00:19:09.757 --> 00:19:11.080
as, as culturally intrusive

00:19:12.307 --> 00:19:14.466
and dismissive as doing

00:19:14.467 --> 00:19:16.897
the opposite, which is universalizing

00:19:16.907 --> 00:19:18.336
and in claiming that everyone should

00:19:18.337 --> 00:19:19.357
testify that way.

00:19:20.077 --> 00:19:21.907
So so far, I imagine

00:19:22.387 --> 00:19:24.846
that this is relatively

00:19:24.847 --> 00:19:26.916
intuitive for you.

00:19:28.357 --> 00:19:30.060
I will just point out, before I move on to

00:19:30.117 --> 00:19:31.656
the next section that there are witnesses

00:19:31.657 --> 00:19:33.876
who comment on, and even dispute, the kind

00:19:33.877 --> 00:19:36.306
of psychotherapeutic nuclear family focus

00:19:36.667 --> 00:19:38.334
that I pointed out in Suzanne's story.

00:19:39.037 --> 00:19:41.136
As just one example, a Shoah Foundation

00:19:41.137 --> 00:19:43.087
witness named Meir Shapiro

00:19:43.477 --> 00:19:45.388
argues with his interviewer's questions

00:19:45.517 --> 00:19:47.166
about his parents, or his interviewers

00:19:47.167 --> 00:19:48.980
asking him for the kind of story that

00:19:49.027 --> 00:19:50.126
Suzanne tells us.

00:19:50.617 --> 00:19:51.696
What did your parents do?

00:19:51.717 --> 00:19:53.287
What kind of games did you play together?

00:19:53.317 --> 00:19:54.317
How did you feel?

00:19:54.397 --> 00:19:56.731
And, and Meir Shapiro answers,

00:19:56.827 --> 00:19:58.586
look, it's just not the way it is now.

00:19:58.597 --> 00:20:00.006
Parents spending a lot of time with their

00:20:00.007 --> 00:20:01.869
children. That's not, that was not the

00:20:02.017 --> 00:20:03.637
case then with me or with anyone.

00:20:03.937 --> 00:20:05.616
Parents keep, kept to themselves and

00:20:05.617 --> 00:20:07.176
children grew up with whatever they could

00:20:07.177 --> 00:20:08.177
get for themselves.

00:20:08.707 --> 00:20:11.077
So that is to say, not all witnesses

00:20:11.167 --> 00:20:13.446
in this English American

00:20:13.537 --> 00:20:14.762
ecology endorse a nuclear

00:20:15.667 --> 00:20:17.916
pam-- A nuclear family paradigm.

00:20:18.217 --> 00:20:20.706
But most respond to it in some fashion,

00:20:20.737 --> 00:20:22.759
even if it's as a direct counterpoint

00:20:23.287 --> 00:20:24.966
when piecing together their stories.

00:20:25.297 --> 00:20:27.536
So this is probably the, the

00:20:28.327 --> 00:20:30.936
kind of rhetorical

00:20:30.937 --> 00:20:32.857
condition, to use Carolyn's phrase,

00:20:33.067 --> 00:20:34.637
that you're most familiar with.

00:20:34.667 --> 00:20:35.916
And now I want to talk about

00:20:37.327 --> 00:20:39.189
the one that I brought in to this book

00:20:39.547 --> 00:20:41.196
that hadn't been researched at all.

00:20:41.227 --> 00:20:43.026
The one that I, in fact, started with.

00:20:43.357 --> 00:20:44.974
I conducted interviews in Yiddish

00:20:45.547 --> 00:20:46.547
in Lithuania.

00:20:46.647 --> 00:20:48.517
And I want to focus on the

00:20:49.177 --> 00:20:50.941
testimony of Doba Rosenberg, who was

00:20:51.007 --> 00:20:52.356
interviewed by the Shoah Foundation

00:20:52.357 --> 00:20:54.078
Institute in Kovne,

00:20:54.547 --> 00:20:57.097
Lithuania, Kaunas, in 1996.

00:20:58.117 --> 00:20:59.539
She was born in 1928.

00:21:02.377 --> 00:21:04.536
Whereas testimonies from

00:21:04.687 --> 00:21:06.606
this English speaking American corpus are

00:21:06.607 --> 00:21:08.273
rich with resources for discussing

00:21:08.737 --> 00:21:10.648
emotional exchanges between parents and

00:21:10.747 --> 00:21:12.396
children, Yiddish-Lithuanian

00:21:12.967 --> 00:21:15.396
testimonies display abundance

00:21:15.607 --> 00:21:16.687
of a different sort.

00:21:17.887 --> 00:21:19.798
They speak a great deal about her broad

00:21:20.227 --> 00:21:21.817
family community matrix.

00:21:22.537 --> 00:21:24.399
Alongside many different categories of

00:21:24.607 --> 00:21:26.367
blood relations, mishpokhe, familie,

00:21:27.427 --> 00:21:28.848
kreyvim, fraynt, all of which

00:21:29.647 --> 00:21:31.057
are synonyms for family.

00:21:31.477 --> 00:21:33.486
Witnesses devote much attention to people

00:21:33.757 --> 00:21:36.547
with the much hazier kind of closeness.

00:21:36.937 --> 00:21:38.701
Friends, acquaintances, townspeople,

00:21:39.277 --> 00:21:40.754
or those they simply call unzere,

00:21:41.407 --> 00:21:42.407
ours.

00:21:42.817 --> 00:21:44.581
As shorthand for this whole semantic

00:21:44.587 --> 00:21:46.547
cluster, I use the term "di eygenen,

00:21:47.067 --> 00:21:48.417
di eygenen," one's own.

00:21:49.587 --> 00:21:51.057
This adjective, crucially, can

00:21:51.807 --> 00:21:53.357
be used alone,

00:21:53.967 --> 00:21:56.186
functioning as a noun or be paired

00:21:56.187 --> 00:21:58.227
with a whole variety of reference.

00:21:59.217 --> 00:22:01.307
That means that one's own, what eygene

00:22:01.677 --> 00:22:04.436
evokes is tellingly left open.

00:22:04.527 --> 00:22:06.386
It can refer to family, friends,

00:22:06.387 --> 00:22:08.102
townspeople, schoolmates, coworkers

00:22:08.997 --> 00:22:10.736
and others with some significant shared

00:22:10.737 --> 00:22:11.737
experience.

00:22:13.737 --> 00:22:15.926
Doba Rozenberg, born

00:22:15.927 --> 00:22:17.876
in Yurberik in 1928.

00:22:18.567 --> 00:22:20.316
Interviewed by the Shoah Foundation.

00:22:21.567 --> 00:22:23.727
She introduces herself on camera

00:22:23.757 --> 00:22:25.678
by declaring "Ikh aleyn

00:22:26.157 --> 00:22:28.196
bin eyn yurberiker." I myself

00:22:28.287 --> 00:22:29.287
am a Yurberiker

00:22:30.447 --> 00:22:31.857
in the present tense.

00:22:32.877 --> 00:22:35.037
So even in

00:22:36.737 --> 00:22:38.156
1996,

00:22:39.237 --> 00:22:41.050
she's discusses herself, she presents

00:22:41.667 --> 00:22:44.277
herself as a part of this local matrix

00:22:44.307 --> 00:22:46.169
in a place that for her doesn't really

00:22:46.197 --> 00:22:48.056
exist anymore. In the present tense, this

00:22:48.057 --> 00:22:50.366
is still the best way to say

00:22:50.367 --> 00:22:51.881
who she is on camera.

00:22:55.407 --> 00:22:57.596
Doba narrates the first days

00:22:57.597 --> 00:22:59.417
of war as they affected the Yuberiker

00:23:00.167 --> 00:23:01.167
collectively.

00:23:01.827 --> 00:23:03.493
She reports on the fate of a broad

00:23:04.257 --> 00:23:06.067
cast of characters such as my mother's

00:23:06.079 --> 00:23:07.639
brother, Uncle Fievel.

00:23:08.427 --> 00:23:10.377
My father's brother, Uncle Max.

00:23:10.767 --> 00:23:12.866
Doba spreads her narrative focus among

00:23:12.867 --> 00:23:13.867
many Yurberiker, but

00:23:15.057 --> 00:23:16.821
does take special care when speaking

00:23:17.337 --> 00:23:18.536
about her parents.

00:23:19.147 --> 00:23:21.206
She's brought to tears when she recounts

00:23:21.207 --> 00:23:23.298
her father shouting, "watch

00:23:23.607 --> 00:23:25.166
out for yourselves, look out for one

00:23:25.167 --> 00:23:27.078
another," as he was caught and led away

00:23:27.327 --> 00:23:29.156
from their house in an early roundup of

00:23:29.157 --> 00:23:30.447
the town's Jewish men.

00:23:31.377 --> 00:23:33.897
However, following her father's capture,

00:23:34.167 --> 00:23:35.897
surprisingly, Doba's

00:23:36.327 --> 00:23:38.296
narrative quickly progresses.

00:23:39.297 --> 00:23:41.096
She tells us that she and her mother found

00:23:41.097 --> 00:23:42.812
shelter in the house of someone she

00:23:43.047 --> 00:23:45.527
describes as, quote, "the neighbor,

00:23:45.537 --> 00:23:47.597
my mother's cousin's husband, Abe

00:23:48.219 --> 00:23:49.219
Vailes."

00:23:49.347 --> 00:23:51.596
So rather than delving into her emotional

00:23:51.597 --> 00:23:53.456
reaction to the loss of her father,

00:23:53.787 --> 00:23:55.502
she shifts her social emphasis onto

00:23:56.007 --> 00:23:57.296
this other relationship.

00:23:58.557 --> 00:24:00.926
She performs a similar shift immediately

00:24:00.927 --> 00:24:03.416
after recalling the capture of her mother.

00:24:03.987 --> 00:24:06.146
Doba was knocked unconscious when her,

00:24:06.237 --> 00:24:08.277
when local

00:24:08.397 --> 00:24:09.818
Lithuanian collaborators took

00:24:10.617 --> 00:24:11.617
away her mother.

00:24:11.877 --> 00:24:13.556
And she says, "When I woke up, I was

00:24:13.557 --> 00:24:15.806
already at the Vailes' with

00:24:15.807 --> 00:24:17.906
Henie, ba henien, at her house.

00:24:18.237 --> 00:24:20.186
My mother's cousin with her kids, of

00:24:20.187 --> 00:24:22.196
course. I screamed and cried and they all

00:24:22.197 --> 00:24:24.056
comforted me." And what's

00:24:24.717 --> 00:24:26.456
especially different for comparing

00:24:27.567 --> 00:24:29.186
the testimony of Suzanne H.

00:24:29.187 --> 00:24:30.196
to Doba Rosenberg?

00:24:30.207 --> 00:24:32.607
You recall that Suzanne H.,

00:24:32.667 --> 00:24:34.887
she really felt

00:24:36.267 --> 00:24:38.031
almost nothing towards her surrogate

00:24:38.727 --> 00:24:40.437
parents. They were unnatural.

00:24:40.467 --> 00:24:41.517
They yelled at her.

00:24:41.607 --> 00:24:43.224
She, she wet her bed when she was

00:24:43.737 --> 00:24:45.027
brought into a new household.

00:24:45.417 --> 00:24:47.666
And Doba actually treats

00:24:47.667 --> 00:24:49.137
this kind of shift as natural.

00:24:49.527 --> 00:24:51.206
And she claims that it was in keeping with

00:24:51.207 --> 00:24:52.766
the way that she was brought up before the

00:24:52.767 --> 00:24:53.767
war.

00:24:54.297 --> 00:24:55.457
She says, "of course, this Mira-Melne

00:24:56.727 --> 00:24:58.526
[Henie's daughter] and my mother were

00:24:58.527 --> 00:24:59.606
always like sisters.

00:24:59.607 --> 00:25:01.377
We'd always been like one family."

00:25:01.977 --> 00:25:03.937
Her acceptance and justification of this

00:25:04.107 --> 00:25:06.116
new family arrangement stands in contrast

00:25:06.867 --> 00:25:09.356
to a wide variety of reactions

00:25:09.357 --> 00:25:11.547
that I found among American witnesses

00:25:11.877 --> 00:25:13.886
who address surrogate parents with

00:25:13.887 --> 00:25:16.077
disappointment or humorous discomfort.

00:25:17.397 --> 00:25:18.524
So Doba has essentially

00:25:19.977 --> 00:25:22.257
become a part of this new family

00:25:22.807 --> 00:25:23.807
in, in Yurberik.

00:25:24.507 --> 00:25:26.666
And they, they take parent

00:25:26.667 --> 00:25:28.706
like responsibility for her and using a

00:25:28.707 --> 00:25:30.626
whole wide variety of connections,

00:25:30.927 --> 00:25:33.057
all of which she, she explicates on,

00:25:33.147 --> 00:25:34.147
on camera.

00:25:34.227 --> 00:25:36.616
Actually in the

00:25:36.627 --> 00:25:38.430
Shoah Foundation archives,

00:25:38.997 --> 00:25:41.036
they have a short informational

00:25:41.037 --> 00:25:42.896
questionnaire which the interviewer is

00:25:42.897 --> 00:25:43.916
supposed to fill out.

00:25:44.457 --> 00:25:46.946
And there's a white

00:25:47.067 --> 00:25:49.027
blank space about this big on one of the

00:25:49.047 --> 00:25:51.416
pages asking for the people named

00:25:51.447 --> 00:25:53.186
in the, in the interview.

00:25:53.517 --> 00:25:55.666
And most of the time, that suffices.

00:25:55.677 --> 00:25:57.386
That's the apparently since that's the

00:25:57.387 --> 00:25:58.527
space that they were allotted.

00:25:58.837 --> 00:26:00.926
And in in Doba's case,

00:26:00.987 --> 00:26:02.849
the interviewer actually had to add on

00:26:03.147 --> 00:26:05.426
two pages in order to list

00:26:05.517 --> 00:26:07.606
all the names that Doba includes.

00:26:07.617 --> 00:26:10.136
And I think that the naming, the inclusion

00:26:10.227 --> 00:26:12.297
of, of so many

00:26:12.777 --> 00:26:14.682
characters is significant, that is

00:26:15.057 --> 00:26:17.337
it expresses an expectation that

00:26:17.547 --> 00:26:19.286
the listener is going to be able to follow

00:26:19.287 --> 00:26:21.027
along. This is relevant information.

00:26:21.057 --> 00:26:23.054
I can follow Doba's mother's cousin's

00:26:23.127 --> 00:26:24.127
brother Abe Vailes...

00:26:24.777 --> 00:26:26.186
So first of all, it says something about

00:26:26.187 --> 00:26:27.412
who her expected audience

00:26:28.377 --> 00:26:29.316
of memory is.

00:26:29.317 --> 00:26:31.346
She's accustomed to telling her story to

00:26:31.347 --> 00:26:33.747
people who know this matrix,

00:26:34.197 --> 00:26:36.747
and it says something about her

00:26:36.897 --> 00:26:38.906
sense of self that it's spread among this

00:26:39.147 --> 00:26:41.126
whole chorus of people,

00:26:41.877 --> 00:26:43.396
that this is, this is not, this

00:26:44.006 --> 00:26:45.966
is not a diversion for her to go into

00:26:46.296 --> 00:26:48.354
this long chain of names, this is actually

00:26:48.486 --> 00:26:49.506
part of who she is.

00:26:50.437 --> 00:26:51.847
So through this long

00:26:52.897 --> 00:26:55.386
chain of people, this cluster

00:26:55.477 --> 00:26:57.469
arrives in the Kovne

00:26:57.847 --> 00:26:58.847
ghetto.

00:26:58.927 --> 00:27:01.506
And upon their arrival, Doba encounters

00:27:01.566 --> 00:27:04.036
a new version of her eygene collective.

00:27:04.086 --> 00:27:06.186
And I'll play you a bit of her

00:27:06.546 --> 00:27:08.766
testimony at this point.

00:27:09.877 --> 00:27:11.076
And pay attention. Pay attention.

00:27:11.077 --> 00:27:13.135
Well. I'll stop in the middle and tell you

00:27:13.296 --> 00:27:14.586
what I want you to pay attention to.

00:27:24.687 --> 00:27:25.226
I just tested it.

00:27:25.227 --> 00:27:26.227
I tested it too, it worked.

00:27:37.587 --> 00:27:38.587
[TESTIMONY IN YIDDISH.]

00:28:31.167 --> 00:28:32.266
I'm just going to pause-- pause for a

00:28:32.267 --> 00:28:34.486
second and point out something

00:28:34.487 --> 00:28:35.761
interesting about the kind

00:28:36.707 --> 00:28:39.136
of socialities that don't interest

00:28:39.467 --> 00:28:41.566
Doba. So the interviewer asked her what

00:28:41.567 --> 00:28:43.607
happened to her, her belongings.

00:28:44.207 --> 00:28:46.006
And she says, you know, I don't know.

00:28:46.037 --> 00:28:47.896
There were some, some station there with

00:28:47.897 --> 00:28:49.966
people. So she's not interested in whether

00:28:49.967 --> 00:28:51.247
her belongings went to the Judenrat.

00:28:51.977 --> 00:28:53.790
Or to some community organization who

00:28:54.227 --> 00:28:55.227
took it away. So

00:28:56.597 --> 00:28:58.246
this is in distinction to Israeli

00:28:58.247 --> 00:28:59.707
testimonies, I'm not going to discuss it.

00:28:59.747 --> 00:29:01.937
She's not really interested

00:29:02.027 --> 00:29:04.156
in formal

00:29:04.487 --> 00:29:06.756
associations of people, formalized

00:29:06.767 --> 00:29:08.866
groups that have names on them, you know,

00:29:09.047 --> 00:29:10.367
political organizations.

00:29:10.617 --> 00:29:11.836
There is not one mention.

00:29:12.227 --> 00:29:13.966
She's not interested in youth groups.

00:29:14.207 --> 00:29:16.336
She's interested specifically in

00:29:16.337 --> 00:29:18.677
this eygene, this informal

00:29:19.007 --> 00:29:21.077
and flexible type of,

00:29:21.467 --> 00:29:23.836
of, of group,

00:29:24.327 --> 00:29:25.876
of bonding. And I'll just play a little

00:29:25.877 --> 00:29:27.616
bit more for you. And do note the number

00:29:27.617 --> 00:29:29.386
of names that come up and also the details

00:29:29.387 --> 00:29:30.586
that she shares about these people, their

00:29:30.587 --> 00:29:31.816
addresses and so forth.

00:29:31.867 --> 00:29:32.867
OK.

00:29:37.987 --> 00:29:38.987
[TESTIMONY IN YIDDISH]

00:30:41.137 --> 00:30:43.537
So note how her

00:30:43.747 --> 00:30:46.026
memory of her bodily life

00:30:46.027 --> 00:30:48.426
is also in concert with her

00:30:48.517 --> 00:30:50.196
notion of belonging.

00:30:51.307 --> 00:30:53.796
She doesn't survive by distinguishing

00:30:53.797 --> 00:30:55.757
herself, or running away from people, or

00:30:55.897 --> 00:30:57.765
being strong. She actually depicts

00:30:57.957 --> 00:30:59.286
survival as a kind of

00:31:00.697 --> 00:31:01.971
cuddling type of activity.

00:31:03.007 --> 00:31:04.866
And just as she says, she details that she

00:31:04.867 --> 00:31:06.596
slept on the floor with her grandmother

00:31:06.597 --> 00:31:08.646
and she goes on and says, who shared a bed

00:31:08.647 --> 00:31:09.937
with whom? So in a sense,

00:31:10.837 --> 00:31:13.236
also surviving

00:31:13.237 --> 00:31:14.237
is is about melding

00:31:15.697 --> 00:31:17.431
in with people in this kind of, through

00:31:18.037 --> 00:31:19.747
these kinds of small motions.

00:31:21.037 --> 00:31:22.605
Her intonation, even while she's

00:31:23.197 --> 00:31:24.897
telling-- I realized that that it's, it's,

00:31:24.997 --> 00:31:26.586
it's hard to watch too much of it when

00:31:26.587 --> 00:31:28.716
you're not when you're not that familiar

00:31:28.717 --> 00:31:30.579
with it precisely because there aren't

00:31:30.847 --> 00:31:33.216
really these big climatic moments

00:31:33.247 --> 00:31:34.716
in her testimony and there aren't really

00:31:34.927 --> 00:31:36.997
what we consider cathartic moments.

00:31:37.327 --> 00:31:39.336
It's really a kind, there's a kind of

00:31:39.697 --> 00:31:41.526
continuous pulse to it.

00:31:41.977 --> 00:31:44.586
And that, too, in a way, echoes

00:31:44.647 --> 00:31:45.676
and amplifies her way

00:31:47.227 --> 00:31:49.377
of presenting belongingness as, as

00:31:49.407 --> 00:31:52.026
eygene, something with these movements,

00:31:52.277 --> 00:31:54.186
and replacements and adjustments, rather

00:31:54.187 --> 00:31:56.256
than a really sharp overturning and

00:31:56.257 --> 00:31:58.596
flipping around the world like we saw with

00:31:59.047 --> 00:32:00.076
Suzanne H.

00:32:00.817 --> 00:32:02.917
This does not mean that Doba's

00:32:03.127 --> 00:32:05.406
testimony is more upbeat or that

00:32:05.407 --> 00:32:07.326
this is a resilience narrative,

00:32:07.547 --> 00:32:09.726
as in opposition

00:32:09.727 --> 00:32:10.836
to a trauma narrative.

00:32:12.247 --> 00:32:14.287
Doba, because Doba keeps

00:32:14.377 --> 00:32:16.566
repopulating the eygene as

00:32:16.567 --> 00:32:18.127
the testimony goes on.

00:32:18.697 --> 00:32:20.510
She actually has to lose more people,

00:32:21.097 --> 00:32:22.986
too. And this is palpable

00:32:23.227 --> 00:32:24.607
in the testimony.

00:32:25.057 --> 00:32:27.336
For instance, while she was in, in the,

00:32:27.367 --> 00:32:29.167
still in the Kovne ghetto, she bonds with

00:32:29.177 --> 00:32:31.372
young cousin's cousin,

00:32:31.497 --> 00:32:32.958
cousin Avivale.

00:32:33.997 --> 00:32:36.546
And when Avivale was taken away, she says,

00:32:37.057 --> 00:32:39.037
"I didn't want to go because this Avivale

00:32:39.487 --> 00:32:41.766
was to me like, how do you say

00:32:41.767 --> 00:32:44.076
it? I loved her very much

00:32:44.077 --> 00:32:46.237
and she loved me." This

00:32:46.477 --> 00:32:48.094
moment is actually one of the few

00:32:48.877 --> 00:32:50.543
in the testimony when Dobis speaks

00:32:50.647 --> 00:32:52.627
explicitly of love,

00:32:52.947 --> 00:32:55.086
hobn holt, throughout the whole

00:32:55.087 --> 00:32:57.156
testimony. So in her emotional

00:32:57.427 --> 00:32:59.586
repertoire, these

00:32:59.587 --> 00:33:02.227
are not instrumental relationships.

00:33:02.257 --> 00:33:03.257
This is love.

00:33:04.327 --> 00:33:06.187
This eygene belonging

00:33:06.647 --> 00:33:07.647
practice.

00:33:07.687 --> 00:33:08.646
It's really a practice.

00:33:08.647 --> 00:33:09.997
It's not a set group of people.

00:33:10.537 --> 00:33:12.756
So even with these pronounced breaks in

00:33:12.757 --> 00:33:14.766
her circle of belonging, the overall

00:33:14.767 --> 00:33:17.196
framework of eygene adjusts and continues

00:33:17.197 --> 00:33:19.236
to organize her social interactions.

00:33:19.957 --> 00:33:22.086
One way in which Doba performs is

00:33:22.117 --> 00:33:23.586
a conceptual adjustment.

00:33:23.617 --> 00:33:25.597
This stretching of the eygene

00:33:25.917 --> 00:33:28.776
network is by loosening her geographic

00:33:28.807 --> 00:33:31.086
criteria of identification.

00:33:31.677 --> 00:33:33.588
While she was in Kovne, Doba considered

00:33:34.207 --> 00:33:35.207
herself a Yurberiker.

00:33:36.007 --> 00:33:38.196
But once she was taken away to

00:33:38.197 --> 00:33:39.477
an Estonian work camp Ereda,

00:33:41.107 --> 00:33:42.846
Doba begins to speak of herself as a

00:33:42.847 --> 00:33:44.925
Kovner and everyone who in Kovne

00:33:45.247 --> 00:33:46.517
is now part of her circle.

00:33:47.017 --> 00:33:48.297
Then, when she's taken away to Stutthof

00:33:49.037 --> 00:33:51.246
about a year later, also the

00:33:51.247 --> 00:33:53.766
prisoners from Ereda are included

00:33:53.767 --> 00:33:54.816
into her circle.

00:33:55.447 --> 00:33:57.393
And there in Kovne--

00:33:57.907 --> 00:33:59.462
I mean, excuse me, in Stutthof,

00:34:00.397 --> 00:34:02.736
she bonds together with this particular

00:34:02.737 --> 00:34:04.577
group of women: Froy

00:34:05.017 --> 00:34:06.756
Shapiro, Mrs. Shapiro, Mrs.

00:34:07.927 --> 00:34:09.357
Karnovishsky and Malka Gempel.

00:34:10.327 --> 00:34:11.517
And these become co-protagonists

00:34:12.847 --> 00:34:14.616
of her story. And she actually tells

00:34:15.067 --> 00:34:16.926
gre-- in great detail what happens to each

00:34:16.927 --> 00:34:19.627
one of them, sharing the narrative

00:34:19.717 --> 00:34:21.816
stage with these other

00:34:21.967 --> 00:34:22.967
individuals.

00:34:23.937 --> 00:34:25.603
The eygene is not just a system of

00:34:25.687 --> 00:34:27.186
inclusion, it's also a system of

00:34:27.187 --> 00:34:28.187
exclusion.

00:34:28.387 --> 00:34:30.004
Doba specifies, for example, that

00:34:30.037 --> 00:34:32.167
prisoners who arrived at Stutthof

00:34:32.347 --> 00:34:34.836
from Romania were also Jewish,

00:34:35.167 --> 00:34:37.527
but she qua-- she qualifies quickly

00:34:37.837 --> 00:34:39.097
zey zaynen gebleyben apart.

00:34:39.967 --> 00:34:41.196
They stayed separately.

00:34:41.257 --> 00:34:42.257
They weren't eygene.

00:34:42.487 --> 00:34:44.437
And she feels this also on her return.

00:34:44.467 --> 00:34:45.997
She returns to Lithuania.

00:34:46.507 --> 00:34:48.366
She depicts the country as a desolate

00:34:48.367 --> 00:34:49.657
place, a place of ruin

00:34:51.127 --> 00:34:53.526
in which Jewish survivors

00:34:53.647 --> 00:34:55.656
regroup according to some new application

00:34:56.357 --> 00:34:57.357
of old ideas.

00:34:58.357 --> 00:35:00.336
She recounts her arrival at this train

00:35:00.337 --> 00:35:01.337
station.

00:35:01.387 --> 00:35:02.707
"We arrived in Vilna.

00:35:02.737 --> 00:35:04.836
The Vilner came and started to ask

00:35:04.867 --> 00:35:07.357
if maybe some of their eygene were there.

00:35:07.777 --> 00:35:09.187
They gave us a place to sleep.

00:35:09.247 --> 00:35:10.986
We stay there a few days and then we moved

00:35:10.987 --> 00:35:11.616
on."

00:35:11.617 --> 00:35:13.283
So Doba seems to understand, share

00:35:13.867 --> 00:35:16.236
a kind of normative

00:35:16.237 --> 00:35:18.187
language with these Vilner

00:35:18.487 --> 00:35:20.046
to understand that they are looking for

00:35:20.047 --> 00:35:21.937
their eygene and she's not one of them.

00:35:22.687 --> 00:35:25.236
She moves on progressively,

00:35:25.237 --> 00:35:27.037
going back to her original site

00:35:27.547 --> 00:35:29.676
of origin and goes back

00:35:29.677 --> 00:35:30.606
to Yurberik.

00:35:30.607 --> 00:35:32.269
And there she finds none other than Abe

00:35:32.270 --> 00:35:34.776
Vailes, the mother's

00:35:34.777 --> 00:35:36.546
cousin's husband, who took her in

00:35:36.907 --> 00:35:38.796
at the very beginning of the war.

00:35:39.187 --> 00:35:41.386
And the way that she describes this

00:35:41.387 --> 00:35:43.426
encounter is truly as a type of

00:35:43.427 --> 00:35:44.427
homecoming.

00:35:45.737 --> 00:35:47.716
She says "Abe spotted me

00:35:47.897 --> 00:35:50.026
like his eygn kind," like his

00:35:50.027 --> 00:35:51.027
own child.

00:35:51.347 --> 00:35:53.297
"He took me in right away

00:35:53.837 --> 00:35:56.050
and he kept me like his eygn

00:35:56.507 --> 00:35:58.426
child, like his own child."

00:35:59.267 --> 00:36:01.167
So Abe and Doba

00:36:01.967 --> 00:36:03.981
reconstitute a household.

00:36:04.517 --> 00:36:06.436
And she lives there up until her wedding

00:36:06.437 --> 00:36:07.816
in 1946,.

00:36:08.297 --> 00:36:10.061
Doba hastens to mention that the man

00:36:10.667 --> 00:36:13.066
she chooses to marry is quote, "also

00:36:13.097 --> 00:36:15.256
a Yurberiker," again, a

00:36:15.257 --> 00:36:17.566
title that remains relevant in the present

00:36:17.567 --> 00:36:19.736
tense in 1996 when she's talking.

00:36:20.207 --> 00:36:22.186
And she also identifies the families that

00:36:22.187 --> 00:36:23.902
her children married into by citing

00:36:24.407 --> 00:36:25.996
micro geographic origin.

00:36:26.387 --> 00:36:28.217
She tells, for example, my daughter

00:36:28.577 --> 00:36:30.707
in law is from Zarasai, which is an

00:36:30.737 --> 00:36:32.207
eastern Lithuanian town.

00:36:33.497 --> 00:36:35.506
Thus, in her postwar and current state of

00:36:35.537 --> 00:36:38.056
being, Doba depicts herself embedded

00:36:38.327 --> 00:36:40.546
in the kind of group in which she began

00:36:40.547 --> 00:36:41.547
her story.

00:36:42.507 --> 00:36:44.236
It is not that Doba's family's story is

00:36:44.237 --> 00:36:46.126
one of resilience again in the testimonies

00:36:46.127 --> 00:36:47.657
from the English American corpus focused

00:36:47.957 --> 00:36:48.957
on loss.

00:36:49.117 --> 00:36:50.587
Doba also depicts postwar life

00:36:51.227 --> 00:36:52.277
as incomplete.

00:36:52.757 --> 00:36:54.676
Most members of her original group have

00:36:54.677 --> 00:36:57.496
been destroyed, but the criteria

00:36:57.527 --> 00:36:59.176
that enable people to connect

00:37:00.257 --> 00:37:01.607
survived the Holocaust.

00:37:04.797 --> 00:37:06.737
So why is this?

00:37:06.927 --> 00:37:08.593
It's a surprising way of narrating

00:37:09.327 --> 00:37:10.716
the Holocaust, to

00:37:11.577 --> 00:37:12.606
claim even implicitly

00:37:14.037 --> 00:37:16.047
this type of continuity.

00:37:17.787 --> 00:37:19.556
I think that in this group of testimonies

00:37:19.557 --> 00:37:21.321
studied here, witnesses formed their

00:37:21.327 --> 00:37:23.517
narrative habits in both the language

00:37:23.547 --> 00:37:25.676
and physical environments of pre-war

00:37:25.677 --> 00:37:27.441
life. And I do believe that physical

00:37:27.897 --> 00:37:30.206
landscape is very important

00:37:30.237 --> 00:37:31.336
in shaping memory.

00:37:31.347 --> 00:37:33.266
It's not only about words and what people

00:37:33.267 --> 00:37:34.676
read in the newspaper and hear their

00:37:34.677 --> 00:37:36.539
friends say, it's also the sights that

00:37:36.717 --> 00:37:38.036
they see out their window.

00:37:38.337 --> 00:37:40.796
And I think that this doubling of

00:37:41.007 --> 00:37:43.286
lingual and physical

00:37:43.917 --> 00:37:46.377
continuity encourages

00:37:46.467 --> 00:37:47.888
a type of semantic continuity

00:37:48.627 --> 00:37:50.776
in their memory and to amplify and

00:37:50.817 --> 00:37:53.546
focus on these types of conceptual

00:37:53.907 --> 00:37:56.096
throughlines from the prewar to the

00:37:56.097 --> 00:37:57.116
postwar period.

00:37:58.977 --> 00:38:00.104
So, again, I, I, I want

00:38:01.107 --> 00:38:02.426
to point out that there are witnesses from

00:38:02.427 --> 00:38:04.240
the same environment who ridicule the

00:38:04.287 --> 00:38:06.236
eygene as a platform of memory.

00:38:06.267 --> 00:38:08.243
I interviewed one woman in Kovne

00:38:09.207 --> 00:38:11.069
named Riva S.

00:38:12.117 --> 00:38:14.276
And she, I learned to talk about the

00:38:14.277 --> 00:38:16.746
eygene. I conducted 47

00:38:16.767 --> 00:38:19.107
interviews and in Yiddish in Lithuania.

00:38:19.137 --> 00:38:20.966
And they taught me to talk like Doba, you

00:38:20.967 --> 00:38:22.077
know, everybody adjusts.

00:38:22.437 --> 00:38:23.996
So I was asking, what was your cousin's

00:38:23.997 --> 00:38:26.055
name, so forth and she had no patience for

00:38:26.157 --> 00:38:27.146
that whatsoever.

00:38:27.147 --> 00:38:29.205
She wanted to discuss and I quote, "public

00:38:29.577 --> 00:38:30.896
life, you know, different Jewish

00:38:30.897 --> 00:38:32.661
organizations on the street." And by

00:38:33.057 --> 00:38:35.007
that, she meant the communist underground.

00:38:35.847 --> 00:38:37.647
She told me nothing about cousins.

00:38:37.707 --> 00:38:39.776
She openly disliked the children from her

00:38:39.787 --> 00:38:41.696
Kovne neighborhood who joined the Zionist

00:38:41.697 --> 00:38:42.697
parties.

00:38:43.137 --> 00:38:45.086
That is to say, the subtlely flexion,

00:38:45.207 --> 00:38:47.187
flexible notion of kinship

00:38:47.457 --> 00:38:49.346
that I've been calling the eygene is not a

00:38:49.347 --> 00:38:51.897
requirement of testimony in this Yiddish

00:38:52.137 --> 00:38:54.116
speaking Lithuanian cluster, but one that

00:38:54.117 --> 00:38:56.077
witnesses have to address in some manner,

00:38:56.127 --> 00:38:57.656
even if in distain.

00:38:58.857 --> 00:39:00.337
So I.

00:39:01.647 --> 00:39:03.776
I am going to conclude my

00:39:03.957 --> 00:39:05.556
talk here in two different ways.

00:39:05.567 --> 00:39:07.136
It's going to sound kind of like a Doors

00:39:07.137 --> 00:39:08.967
song has, you know, this ending and then

00:39:09.027 --> 00:39:10.027
it has that ending.

00:39:11.247 --> 00:39:13.207
One of the, one of my conclusions is, is

00:39:13.497 --> 00:39:14.966
a major claim of my books.

00:39:14.967 --> 00:39:16.756
I really wanted to get it out there and,

00:39:17.427 --> 00:39:18.427
and have you hear it.

00:39:18.447 --> 00:39:20.756
And then another of my conclusions

00:39:21.057 --> 00:39:22.976
relates to the contribution that

00:39:23.907 --> 00:39:26.306
Yale scholars made,

00:39:26.577 --> 00:39:28.377
specifically one Yale scholar made

00:39:28.737 --> 00:39:30.446
in informing my research.

00:39:31.227 --> 00:39:33.536
So what does it matter

00:39:33.537 --> 00:39:34.816
what I've pointed out here?

00:39:34.827 --> 00:39:36.846
You know. OK. So those one

00:39:37.167 --> 00:39:39.226
testimonies about continuity and one, you

00:39:39.227 --> 00:39:41.517
know, has a specific point of break.

00:39:42.177 --> 00:39:44.486
And I think that it's more than just--

00:39:45.537 --> 00:39:47.399
it's more, it says something about the

00:39:47.577 --> 00:39:49.386
event itself and it relates, this

00:39:49.407 --> 00:39:51.122
different distinction relates to an

00:39:51.387 --> 00:39:53.426
ongoing question that's been kicked around

00:39:53.427 --> 00:39:55.338
in Holocaust studies for quite a while.

00:39:55.437 --> 00:39:57.656
And that is, is the

00:39:57.657 --> 00:40:00.026
Holocaust, was the Holocaust a catastrophe

00:40:00.057 --> 00:40:01.796
in the strict sense of the word?

00:40:01.827 --> 00:40:03.738
As you all know, since you're smart and

00:40:03.807 --> 00:40:04.807
your Yale.

00:40:05.817 --> 00:40:08.006
So I, I, I don't need

00:40:08.007 --> 00:40:10.136
to tell you that catastrophe doesn't just

00:40:10.137 --> 00:40:11.852
mean a mass amount of human carnage

00:40:12.537 --> 00:40:14.397
and suffering. Catastrophe

00:40:14.757 --> 00:40:16.676
means, this is from Oxford English

00:40:16.677 --> 00:40:18.343
Dictionary, an event that produces

00:40:18.837 --> 00:40:20.967
a subversion of the order of things.

00:40:21.177 --> 00:40:22.745
A catastrophe is a dark cultural

00:40:23.397 --> 00:40:25.347
revolution in a sense.

00:40:25.827 --> 00:40:28.287
And that

00:40:28.767 --> 00:40:30.836
claim, I

00:40:30.927 --> 00:40:32.996
think people go back and forth about it on

00:40:32.997 --> 00:40:33.997
an empirical level.

00:40:34.017 --> 00:40:36.236
If it is if they can prove, you know, some

00:40:36.237 --> 00:40:37.237
certain amount

00:40:38.547 --> 00:40:40.557
of cultural change that would determine,

00:40:40.587 --> 00:40:41.756
which would answer this question

00:40:41.757 --> 00:40:43.646
sufficiently. And I actually think that

00:40:43.647 --> 00:40:45.926
this question is open and that

00:40:46.347 --> 00:40:48.566
the postwar setting of memory

00:40:48.567 --> 00:40:50.667
completes the meaning of the event

00:40:51.027 --> 00:40:52.448
and gives shape to whether or

00:40:53.157 --> 00:40:54.556
not this is a catastrophe or not.

00:40:54.867 --> 00:40:57.296
So in Suzanne's testimony and others like

00:40:57.297 --> 00:40:59.537
hers, there is a distinct moment,

00:40:59.967 --> 00:41:01.886
as you recall, comes from her body

00:41:02.307 --> 00:41:04.796
in which things broke

00:41:04.947 --> 00:41:07.136
and they had to be rebuilt in a new way.

00:41:07.167 --> 00:41:09.225
So it's a catastrophe on the most intimate

00:41:09.537 --> 00:41:12.026
bodily personal level

00:41:12.027 --> 00:41:13.840
for her and then expands out into her

00:41:14.307 --> 00:41:16.406
world view. She changed her language.

00:41:16.677 --> 00:41:18.687
She changed her place of, of, of

00:41:18.777 --> 00:41:20.516
belonging. She changed the way that she's

00:41:20.517 --> 00:41:21.517
Jewish.

00:41:21.567 --> 00:41:24.207
And I don't think that catastrophe

00:41:24.237 --> 00:41:26.756
is an accurate way to describe

00:41:26.757 --> 00:41:28.374
the way that Doba and some others

00:41:28.917 --> 00:41:31.107
in her ecology narrate the Holocaust.

00:41:31.407 --> 00:41:33.897
That is, they describe no less suff--

00:41:33.977 --> 00:41:35.349
of suffering and carnage and

00:41:36.147 --> 00:41:37.466
loss and injustice.

00:41:37.767 --> 00:41:39.727
But they do so through a very surprising

00:41:40.227 --> 00:41:41.305
lens of conceptual and

00:41:42.477 --> 00:41:44.227
cultural continuity.

00:41:44.547 --> 00:41:45.547
So it's mass

00:41:46.767 --> 00:41:49.466
violence, massive

00:41:49.557 --> 00:41:50.831
injustice, mass wrong, but

00:41:51.807 --> 00:41:54.236
not catastrophe in the sense of

00:41:54.417 --> 00:41:56.475
a dark cultural revolution or a subversion

00:41:57.057 --> 00:41:58.167
of the order of things.

00:41:58.467 --> 00:42:00.416
I know that that's a surprising claim to

00:42:00.417 --> 00:42:02.127
make, but I think that that's something

00:42:02.217 --> 00:42:03.638
that we should respect, comes

00:42:04.557 --> 00:42:06.586
from outside of our centers of,

00:42:06.977 --> 00:42:09.207
of cultural norms

00:42:09.237 --> 00:42:11.006
and what we're most familiar with.

00:42:11.427 --> 00:42:12.848
And this is a part of, of, of

00:42:13.557 --> 00:42:15.076
this era of translation when we

00:42:15.807 --> 00:42:18.297
leave our our comfort zones.

00:42:18.567 --> 00:42:20.516
We have to be ready to accept new things.

00:42:20.517 --> 00:42:22.586
And I think that that's the most beautiful

00:42:22.587 --> 00:42:24.776
achievements of Holocaust

00:42:24.987 --> 00:42:26.310
testimonial projects of the

00:42:27.177 --> 00:42:29.487
20th century, was that they,

00:42:29.667 --> 00:42:31.578
you know, different organizations, also

00:42:31.647 --> 00:42:32.897
the Fortunoff organization, went

00:42:33.897 --> 00:42:36.056
abroad and they made these surprising

00:42:36.057 --> 00:42:38.336
discoveries. They didn't just record

00:42:38.337 --> 00:42:39.897
more and more of the same.

00:42:39.957 --> 00:42:41.937
They, they have these archives with,

00:42:42.137 --> 00:42:44.456
with, with surprising inflections

00:42:44.457 --> 00:42:46.226
of, of how to see the Holocaust.

00:42:46.647 --> 00:42:48.509
So my second conclusion, as I promised

00:42:48.607 --> 00:42:50.577
you, the Doors style ending,

00:42:51.207 --> 00:42:53.756
comes from a response

00:42:53.997 --> 00:42:56.576
that the late Geoffrey Hartman offered

00:42:56.907 --> 00:42:58.466
when hearing my work.

00:42:59.127 --> 00:43:01.286
Describing what I was

00:43:01.287 --> 00:43:03.116
then writing my dissertation on,

00:43:03.617 --> 00:43:05.456
I think that our encounter was back in

00:43:05.457 --> 00:43:07.616
2010, I told him that

00:43:07.617 --> 00:43:09.657
I wanted to explore the differences

00:43:09.687 --> 00:43:11.353
between clusters of testimony that

00:43:11.937 --> 00:43:13.797
relate to language in place.

00:43:14.187 --> 00:43:16.467
And I unfolded my basic thesis to him,

00:43:16.497 --> 00:43:18.065
as I have in this lecture today,

00:43:18.927 --> 00:43:21.147
about different genres of testimony,

00:43:21.477 --> 00:43:23.846
different moral and rhetorical emphases

00:43:23.847 --> 00:43:25.758
that arose in these different ecologies

00:43:26.187 --> 00:43:27.187
I was studying.

00:43:27.807 --> 00:43:30.116
And Hartman's response

00:43:30.957 --> 00:43:32.577
was quite surprising.

00:43:33.147 --> 00:43:35.816
He listened to my thoughts on testimonial

00:43:35.817 --> 00:43:37.238
difference and then corrected

00:43:38.187 --> 00:43:39.187
me.

00:43:39.507 --> 00:43:42.446
He said, no, it is not difference

00:43:42.537 --> 00:43:44.636
that you are actually studying.

00:43:44.967 --> 00:43:47.156
It's something that is the opposite of

00:43:47.157 --> 00:43:48.157
difference.

00:43:48.687 --> 00:43:50.727
And of course, he was right.

00:43:51.717 --> 00:43:53.775
Scholars have always known each individual

00:43:54.357 --> 00:43:57.026
Holocaust testimony, which is accompanied

00:43:57.027 --> 00:43:58.693
by a unique individual interviewer

00:43:59.637 --> 00:44:01.316
will produce something different.

00:44:02.397 --> 00:44:04.766
What the comparison shows

00:44:05.517 --> 00:44:07.477
is actually how there are communities of

00:44:07.767 --> 00:44:09.090
truth telling, how clusters

00:44:10.107 --> 00:44:12.116
of people who are bonded together through

00:44:12.267 --> 00:44:14.426
language and place build a

00:44:14.427 --> 00:44:16.437
common language of memory.

00:44:17.757 --> 00:44:19.374
And they build a shared method of

00:44:20.097 --> 00:44:21.959
going into this adventure of testimony

00:44:22.647 --> 00:44:23.647
together.

00:44:24.567 --> 00:44:26.906
And contrary to some, I do not see

00:44:26.907 --> 00:44:29.236
this shared language of testimony within

00:44:29.237 --> 00:44:31.526
each ecology as in any way

00:44:31.587 --> 00:44:33.656
a downside or a sign of

00:44:33.717 --> 00:44:34.977
inauthenticity.

00:44:35.907 --> 00:44:36.986
Quite the opposite.

00:44:37.797 --> 00:44:40.197
Without some common frameworks,

00:44:40.917 --> 00:44:42.632
without those shared rhetorical and

00:44:42.687 --> 00:44:44.696
somatic moves, survivors would have never

00:44:45.327 --> 00:44:48.236
had the social support for testifying

00:44:48.237 --> 00:44:49.377
in the first place.

00:44:49.647 --> 00:44:52.196
So if we think of Shoshana Felman

00:44:52.197 --> 00:44:54.327
and Dori Laub's work on testimony

00:44:54.357 --> 00:44:56.170
as an I-Thou relationship, there's no

00:44:56.577 --> 00:44:58.737
way that people could say I and thou

00:44:59.307 --> 00:45:00.973
without some sense of community in

00:45:01.497 --> 00:45:03.327
words and in norms

00:45:03.677 --> 00:45:05.245
and and it's almost like some of

00:45:05.877 --> 00:45:08.228
these shared aspects,

00:45:08.377 --> 00:45:10.435
these, these shared two point of testimony

00:45:10.437 --> 00:45:12.626
are like rituals of inclusion and ways

00:45:12.627 --> 00:45:14.996
of signaling to one another that they have

00:45:15.777 --> 00:45:18.207
a somewhat safe space of memory together.

00:45:18.537 --> 00:45:20.947
So this is not about difference.

00:45:20.957 --> 00:45:22.887
This is about actually

00:45:23.397 --> 00:45:26.066
really surprising ways in which people

00:45:26.127 --> 00:45:28.017
choose to, to share

00:45:28.527 --> 00:45:29.967
their ways of remembering.

00:45:30.447 --> 00:45:31.946
So thank you very much.

00:45:40.546 --> 00:45:42.217
Go back to the general things that so

00:45:42.287 --> 00:45:43.287
nicely.

00:45:43.827 --> 00:45:44.877
So.

00:45:55.477 --> 00:45:56.477
Thank you.

00:45:56.707 --> 00:45:57.707
Sorry.

00:46:00.137 --> 00:46:01.396
Deborah, did you want to come to the

00:46:01.397 --> 00:46:02.397
podium? OK.

00:46:12.537 --> 00:46:13.537
OK.

00:46:13.677 --> 00:46:14.677
Very good.

00:46:16.917 --> 00:46:17.917
So before we

00:46:19.167 --> 00:46:21.176
get to the, before we get to the question

00:46:21.447 --> 00:46:23.816
and answer part

00:46:23.937 --> 00:46:25.617
of our time together,

00:46:27.007 --> 00:46:29.967
I, I have the privilege

00:46:30.477 --> 00:46:32.667
of drilling down

00:46:32.997 --> 00:46:35.517
on some of the things that Hannah has been

00:46:36.057 --> 00:46:37.057
discussing.

00:46:37.707 --> 00:46:39.927
And I should say straight away.

00:46:40.497 --> 00:46:41.869
I was one, I was an external

00:46:42.897 --> 00:46:45.417
reader for her dissertation

00:46:45.687 --> 00:46:48.146
on which this book is based.

00:46:48.537 --> 00:46:50.907
And I thought, wow,

00:46:51.267 --> 00:46:52.757
that's a brain on two feet.

00:46:54.147 --> 00:46:56.727
And then the book was

00:46:57.627 --> 00:46:59.756
accepted by Yale University Press,

00:46:59.757 --> 00:47:01.736
or rather, it came to me

00:47:01.887 --> 00:47:03.296
as a manuscript.

00:47:03.777 --> 00:47:05.590
And that was really a pleasure to see

00:47:06.177 --> 00:47:08.397
how it had changed

00:47:08.727 --> 00:47:11.127
from a dissertation to

00:47:11.157 --> 00:47:13.617
becoming a manuscript for publication.

00:47:14.037 --> 00:47:15.656
And again, I thought,

00:47:16.167 --> 00:47:17.245
wow, that's a brain on

00:47:18.357 --> 00:47:19.357
two feet.

00:47:20.007 --> 00:47:22.077
I think that this is just

00:47:22.347 --> 00:47:23.636
wonderful work.

00:47:24.117 --> 00:47:26.756
And so I want to use this time

00:47:26.757 --> 00:47:28.031
to learn more about it and

00:47:28.887 --> 00:47:31.029
to learn more about Hannah's thoughts

00:47:32.137 --> 00:47:33.506
on how she did her work.

00:47:34.317 --> 00:47:36.081
So the first issue that I would like

00:47:36.537 --> 00:47:39.177
to raise, not surprisingly,

00:47:39.567 --> 00:47:40.567
is gender.

00:47:42.057 --> 00:47:44.276
As we saw the two people

00:47:44.397 --> 00:47:45.475
on whom Hannah focused

00:47:46.617 --> 00:47:49.107
her lecture today, both of them are

00:47:49.227 --> 00:47:50.227
women.

00:47:51.087 --> 00:47:53.307
She also mentioned that

00:47:53.457 --> 00:47:55.319
there was a difference between the way

00:47:55.437 --> 00:47:57.057
Suzanne H.

00:47:57.177 --> 00:47:59.336
and Mayer talked

00:47:59.457 --> 00:48:01.407
about their families,

00:48:01.827 --> 00:48:03.747
that she, Suzanne H.

00:48:03.777 --> 00:48:04.843
talked about family, and

00:48:05.937 --> 00:48:08.377
relations, and emotional history,

00:48:08.907 --> 00:48:10.647
while he did not.

00:48:11.217 --> 00:48:12.537
So I would just wonder,

00:48:13.377 --> 00:48:15.596
if you would be so kind as

00:48:15.597 --> 00:48:18.296
to talk to us about the role of gender

00:48:18.327 --> 00:48:20.116
as you see it in this work.

00:48:20.657 --> 00:48:22.496
Okay, I'm looking it up, because, I want

00:48:22.497 --> 00:48:24.147
to I want to use...

00:48:24.507 --> 00:48:25.507
So

00:48:28.707 --> 00:48:29.816
I think that you're right.

00:48:29.877 --> 00:48:32.006
First of all, over the years, I was

00:48:32.007 --> 00:48:34.366
really resistant to this suggestion

00:48:34.397 --> 00:48:36.596
that came from you in looking

00:48:36.597 --> 00:48:38.906
at this and it's, it's always

00:48:38.907 --> 00:48:41.756
a great, it's a great pleasure

00:48:41.757 --> 00:48:43.707
to say I was wrong.

00:48:44.247 --> 00:48:46.526
I don't know how many other people in this

00:48:46.527 --> 00:48:48.356
room have gotten that privilege, but it's

00:48:48.357 --> 00:48:49.357
a really big one.

00:48:49.647 --> 00:48:51.946
And so this

00:48:51.957 --> 00:48:54.206
could have been a whole dissertation

00:48:54.207 --> 00:48:56.036
book, could have written been written just

00:48:56.037 --> 00:48:57.458
about gender, and gender, not

00:48:58.167 --> 00:48:59.517
in the sense of the

00:49:00.327 --> 00:49:01.647
biological

00:49:03.357 --> 00:49:06.087
equipment of each individual

00:49:07.287 --> 00:49:09.207
survivor, but rather

00:49:11.037 --> 00:49:13.196
gender associated means of

00:49:13.197 --> 00:49:15.206
memory. You know, some else actually came

00:49:15.477 --> 00:49:17.176
up yesterday to that, you know.

00:49:17.217 --> 00:49:18.767
Yes. Are you, are you an agent?

00:49:19.407 --> 00:49:21.447
Are you strong or are you

00:49:21.837 --> 00:49:23.607
someone who focuses on

00:49:24.237 --> 00:49:27.207
his or her suffering

00:49:27.237 --> 00:49:28.287
and victimhood?

00:49:28.347 --> 00:49:30.056
And that's, that is gendered.

00:49:30.087 --> 00:49:31.606
You're right. But it's not just

00:49:32.247 --> 00:49:33.247
for women.

00:49:33.567 --> 00:49:35.457
And I think that there is something

00:49:35.757 --> 00:49:38.366
feminine that's more available

00:49:38.637 --> 00:49:40.947
to witnesses in the

00:49:41.697 --> 00:49:43.559
English American ecology for different

00:49:44.127 --> 00:49:44.636
reasons.

00:49:44.637 --> 00:49:46.136
And I just want to share with you one

00:49:46.137 --> 00:49:47.247
example of that.

00:49:47.667 --> 00:49:48.667
From

00:49:50.907 --> 00:49:52.279
a test, a witness named Jack

00:49:53.157 --> 00:49:55.280
Arnel who is a Shoah

00:49:55.417 --> 00:49:56.787
Foundation witness.

00:49:56.937 --> 00:49:58.766
And I'll offer you a quote, because I do

00:49:58.767 --> 00:50:00.656
want you to know there's actually stuff in

00:50:00.657 --> 00:50:02.816
the book that I didn't present

00:50:02.817 --> 00:50:03.817
today.

00:50:05.037 --> 00:50:06.605
I want the audience to know that

00:50:07.197 --> 00:50:07.796
there's...

00:50:07.797 --> 00:50:08.986
So he's, he's.

00:50:09.267 --> 00:50:10.537
This is in a different chapter.

00:50:10.557 --> 00:50:11.619
This is about the victim-perpetrator

00:50:12.327 --> 00:50:13.327
encounter.

00:50:13.407 --> 00:50:16.046
And he had-- Jack Arnel, he's describing

00:50:16.047 --> 00:50:17.786
the moment in which he was, he and his

00:50:17.787 --> 00:50:19.845
family and his community were evicted from

00:50:19.857 --> 00:50:21.986
their homes and forced into the Vilna

00:50:22.017 --> 00:50:22.946
ghetto.

00:50:22.947 --> 00:50:25.977
And this is an opportunity to describe

00:50:26.397 --> 00:50:27.897
the perpetrator. And he

00:50:29.157 --> 00:50:31.227
elects not to describe the perpetrator,

00:50:31.257 --> 00:50:32.257
but something else.

00:50:32.307 --> 00:50:33.866
And he says, quote, "There are Polish and

00:50:33.867 --> 00:50:35.426
Lithuanians who would jump on people and

00:50:35.427 --> 00:50:37.557
grab their bundles, sickly people,

00:50:37.617 --> 00:50:38.936
the contents sprawled out.

00:50:39.477 --> 00:50:41.192
It is a scene that has made a very,

00:50:41.727 --> 00:50:43.346
very big impression on me.

00:50:43.767 --> 00:50:46.196
I just walk around with a big pain

00:50:46.227 --> 00:50:47.227
in my heart."

00:50:47.457 --> 00:50:49.647
So the emphasis of that

00:50:50.097 --> 00:50:52.247
moment for Jack Arnel

00:50:53.277 --> 00:50:56.546
is also on the emotional

00:50:56.787 --> 00:50:58.736
stakes. The-- what the imprint that it's

00:50:58.737 --> 00:51:00.926
left on him, which is, you know, the very,

00:51:01.257 --> 00:51:02.923
it's a very Freudian notion of the

00:51:03.417 --> 00:51:04.916
self that he's built out of these

00:51:04.917 --> 00:51:05.917
imprints.

00:51:05.937 --> 00:51:07.617
And that's what he's interested in.

00:51:07.947 --> 00:51:09.907
And it is, it is effeminate in a way and

00:51:10.077 --> 00:51:11.187
he's OK with that.

00:51:11.217 --> 00:51:13.466
I mean, he',s he's, he's going

00:51:13.467 --> 00:51:15.186
there. And I think that that is,

00:51:16.517 --> 00:51:18.556
that type of, of, of

00:51:19.247 --> 00:51:21.256
effeminate memory is maybe less available

00:51:21.557 --> 00:51:22.816
in the other ecologies, but there are

00:51:22.817 --> 00:51:24.496
other things that are that are more

00:51:24.497 --> 00:51:26.360
available. So for instance,

00:51:26.657 --> 00:51:28.096
so, again, if we're talking about,

00:51:29.207 --> 00:51:30.677
you know-- I'll

00:51:31.457 --> 00:51:32.716
leave it, I'll leave it at that.

00:51:32.747 --> 00:51:33.747
But again, you

00:51:34.907 --> 00:51:37.036
know, there are all of these different

00:51:37.157 --> 00:51:38.480
axes of comparison that one

00:51:39.377 --> 00:51:40.377
could take up.

00:51:40.547 --> 00:51:42.197
So gender is one of them.

00:51:42.527 --> 00:51:44.147
Language and place is another.

00:51:44.327 --> 00:51:46.287
Difference over time is another one that

00:51:46.577 --> 00:51:47.957
we heard about last night.

00:51:48.587 --> 00:51:49.763
And my object in the, in

00:51:50.807 --> 00:51:52.179
the book is not to overpower

00:51:53.207 --> 00:51:55.336
any other, other axes

00:51:55.337 --> 00:51:56.746
of difference, but to propose this

00:51:56.747 --> 00:51:58.187
framework as an important one.

00:51:58.637 --> 00:52:00.497
And also, the two intersect.

00:52:00.527 --> 00:52:02.866
So gender difference in the Israeli

00:52:02.867 --> 00:52:04.729
context is really different, different

00:52:04.937 --> 00:52:06.586
than gender difference in the American

00:52:06.587 --> 00:52:08.235
context or in the Lithuanian context.

00:52:08.927 --> 00:52:10.914
In the Israeli context,

00:52:11.057 --> 00:52:13.306
a really important part of surviving

00:52:13.307 --> 00:52:16.127
the Holocaust is becoming strong,

00:52:16.217 --> 00:52:18.096
making a nation of strength.

00:52:18.527 --> 00:52:20.095
And that, part of that narrative

00:52:20.687 --> 00:52:23.056
is about serving in the army,

00:52:23.057 --> 00:52:25.336
you know, and and that's not available

00:52:25.337 --> 00:52:27.046
to the same degree to the women who

00:52:27.047 --> 00:52:28.396
witnessed. So they have to come up with

00:52:28.397 --> 00:52:30.406
all of these different jury-rig narrative

00:52:30.977 --> 00:52:33.196
patches to make up for the fact that

00:52:33.197 --> 00:52:35.296
they can't participate in that part of

00:52:35.527 --> 00:52:36.826
the narrative that they want to

00:52:36.827 --> 00:52:37.827
participate in.

00:52:38.117 --> 00:52:40.247
So gender is there and it overlays

00:52:40.577 --> 00:52:42.076
with these other differences.

00:52:42.107 --> 00:52:43.106
And.

00:52:43.107 --> 00:52:45.496
But I go back to my original

00:52:45.497 --> 00:52:47.086
assertion that I was wrong and you were

00:52:47.087 --> 00:52:48.087
right.

00:52:48.477 --> 00:52:49.477
We were both right.

00:52:51.057 --> 00:52:53.216
So I want to I I would like

00:52:53.217 --> 00:52:55.196
you to stay on that subject

00:52:55.467 --> 00:52:57.867
of these different axes

00:52:58.317 --> 00:52:59.689
and you are-- and of course,

00:53:00.687 --> 00:53:03.386
your focus is on language

00:53:03.897 --> 00:53:05.727
and on space,

00:53:06.117 --> 00:53:07.136
on geography.

00:53:09.507 --> 00:53:10.977
I was alerted to this question

00:53:11.847 --> 00:53:14.036
of language by the scholar

00:53:14.277 --> 00:53:15.277
Robert Melson.

00:53:15.567 --> 00:53:18.026
Bob Melson, who is a child

00:53:18.027 --> 00:53:19.840
survivor of the Holocaust, went on to

00:53:20.037 --> 00:53:21.836
become a political scientist,

00:53:22.387 --> 00:53:24.596
is professor emeritus at Purdue,

00:53:25.107 --> 00:53:26.107
and has thought

00:53:27.387 --> 00:53:29.157
about and spoken about

00:53:29.637 --> 00:53:31.707
how he

00:53:31.797 --> 00:53:32.924
is a slightly different

00:53:34.227 --> 00:53:36.986
person when speaking

00:53:36.987 --> 00:53:38.937
his native Polish

00:53:39.597 --> 00:53:42.057
as compared with speaking

00:53:42.207 --> 00:53:44.696
English, even though his language

00:53:44.697 --> 00:53:46.886
of literacy, and adulthood,

00:53:47.277 --> 00:53:49.557
and love and family,

00:53:49.617 --> 00:53:51.866
and culture, all of those

00:53:52.257 --> 00:53:53.607
are English.

00:53:54.237 --> 00:53:56.336
So I wonder if you could

00:53:56.877 --> 00:53:59.437
try to tease out the two

00:54:00.087 --> 00:54:01.087
for us.

00:54:01.287 --> 00:54:03.656
So, for instance, do you,

00:54:04.227 --> 00:54:06.326
can you speak about, and maybe not,

00:54:07.467 --> 00:54:10.076
people whose language

00:54:10.077 --> 00:54:12.477
remains the same, but

00:54:12.717 --> 00:54:15.116
geography changes?

00:54:15.317 --> 00:54:17.426
Anyway, I would just love you to

00:54:17.427 --> 00:54:18.236
tease out that.

00:54:18.237 --> 00:54:19.413
Sure. So I won't go into

00:54:20.397 --> 00:54:22.557
too much, you know, of this--

00:54:23.457 --> 00:54:24.457
these kinds of

00:54:25.647 --> 00:54:27.407
individual comparisons and tracks,

00:54:27.837 --> 00:54:29.216
because then I'm, you know, making

00:54:30.057 --> 00:54:31.196
an Excel chart for you.

00:54:31.227 --> 00:54:33.026
But I do want to, I want to say one, one

00:54:33.027 --> 00:54:34.077
thing. So, yes.

00:54:34.557 --> 00:54:37.257
You know, language, languages,

00:54:37.797 --> 00:54:39.116
and again, I, I,

00:54:40.137 --> 00:54:42.296
I go back to the notion of the

00:54:42.297 --> 00:54:44.577
language, the conceit

00:54:44.607 --> 00:54:47.066
of an ecology and resources,

00:54:47.097 --> 00:54:50.127
because I'm not talking about limitations.

00:54:50.157 --> 00:54:52.466
I don't think that this is about limiting

00:54:52.467 --> 00:54:53.986
what you can say. Quite the opposite.

00:54:53.997 --> 00:54:56.156
I think that this is hard enough to talk

00:54:56.157 --> 00:54:57.578
about that we need resources.

00:54:57.667 --> 00:55:00.026
So the resources that come from

00:55:00.057 --> 00:55:02.276
language help people to

00:55:02.277 --> 00:55:03.686
say what they want to say rather than

00:55:03.687 --> 00:55:04.877
limiting who they can be.

00:55:04.997 --> 00:55:06.467
And but, you know, if we think

00:55:07.227 --> 00:55:08.937
about Mikhail

00:55:09.697 --> 00:55:12.267
Bakhtin's notion of language

00:55:12.567 --> 00:55:13.567
and that,

00:55:15.177 --> 00:55:16.587
that every

00:55:17.517 --> 00:55:19.736
expression is

00:55:20.067 --> 00:55:21.627
a story, it's an intertext

00:55:23.217 --> 00:55:24.295
that's been compressed

00:55:25.377 --> 00:55:26.516
into those words.

00:55:26.937 --> 00:55:29.546
Every idiom that we use

00:55:29.667 --> 00:55:30.687
has memory.

00:55:31.887 --> 00:55:33.267
It has references.

00:55:33.597 --> 00:55:34.871
So when we enter into that

00:55:35.787 --> 00:55:37.453
intertext of one language, we have

00:55:38.037 --> 00:55:40.377
a different set of memories.

00:55:40.407 --> 00:55:42.716
We have a different set of, of

00:55:42.957 --> 00:55:44.237
imagined audiences.

00:55:44.247 --> 00:55:46.286
And so it stands to reason that we would

00:55:46.287 --> 00:55:48.176
be somewhat different people in different

00:55:48.177 --> 00:55:49.177
languages.

00:55:49.587 --> 00:55:51.204
Regarding language in place, when

00:55:51.747 --> 00:55:54.266
I was doing my work in Lithuania,

00:55:54.627 --> 00:55:56.906
I began the project by

00:55:56.907 --> 00:55:58.526
interviewing people in Lithuania.

00:55:59.637 --> 00:56:01.796
I worked with a sociolinguist named

00:56:01.797 --> 00:56:03.986
Dovid Katz and he taught me something very

00:56:03.987 --> 00:56:05.727
important. He said that once

00:56:06.687 --> 00:56:09.897
language has been transplanted

00:56:09.987 --> 00:56:11.906
in the long term, not, you know, for two

00:56:11.907 --> 00:56:13.346
weeks or something, it actually becomes a

00:56:13.347 --> 00:56:14.727
slightly different language.

00:56:15.927 --> 00:56:18.363
So this is especially true

00:56:19.087 --> 00:56:21.176
of Yiddish in postwar

00:56:21.237 --> 00:56:22.237
America.

00:56:23.007 --> 00:56:25.077
They came here, Yiddish speakers

00:56:25.227 --> 00:56:26.997
came here, met with

00:56:27.447 --> 00:56:28.976
a Yiddish speaking community who had

00:56:29.037 --> 00:56:31.796
already developed their dialect

00:56:31.797 --> 00:56:33.866
of Yiddish, and they had better pick up on

00:56:33.867 --> 00:56:35.457
it or they wouldn't be able to speak.

00:56:36.507 --> 00:56:38.877
Isaac Bashevis Singer has a famous

00:56:38.967 --> 00:56:41.306
essay about Yiddish in postwar

00:56:41.307 --> 00:56:42.728
America in which he says, you

00:56:43.457 --> 00:56:44.927
know, mir zeynen geganen zum drugstore

00:56:46.387 --> 00:56:48.053
un dernokh hob ikh gegessen an ice

00:56:48.537 --> 00:56:50.727
cream. And he basically shows how much

00:56:51.057 --> 00:56:52.919
American English has been incorporated

00:56:53.007 --> 00:56:54.176
into the Yiddish idiom.

00:56:54.177 --> 00:56:55.496
And it's almost the new, it's a new

00:56:55.497 --> 00:56:57.506
dialect, is a new language.

00:56:57.507 --> 00:56:59.937
So so I think,

00:56:59.967 --> 00:57:01.747
and they had an literature and a

00:57:01.797 --> 00:57:03.877
newspaper, every-- it was very developed.

00:57:03.897 --> 00:57:06.596
And so my finding

00:57:06.597 --> 00:57:08.627
was that those who moved

00:57:09.177 --> 00:57:10.796
immediately after the war

00:57:11.427 --> 00:57:12.427
to the U.S.

00:57:12.447 --> 00:57:15.026
and for whatever reason, chose to testify

00:57:15.027 --> 00:57:17.066
in Yiddish years later from the U.S.,

00:57:17.307 --> 00:57:18.927
they were speaking American Yiddish.

00:57:18.937 --> 00:57:20.734
It was a different web of intersects.

00:57:21.077 --> 00:57:23.017
It was a different, it was a different, it

00:57:23.057 --> 00:57:24.506
was something else than the Yiddish that

00:57:24.507 --> 00:57:25.507
stayed in Lithuania.

00:57:25.767 --> 00:57:27.384
Now that those who, who emigrated

00:57:27.957 --> 00:57:30.296
much later, like, you know, with the wave

00:57:30.297 --> 00:57:32.427
in the 70s or the 90s,

00:57:32.487 --> 00:57:34.886
I mean, there is one testimony

00:57:34.887 --> 00:57:37.257
in Israel that in Yiddish,

00:57:37.287 --> 00:57:38.904
where he had just moved to Israel

00:57:39.417 --> 00:57:41.277
like six months before he was interviewed.

00:57:41.487 --> 00:57:43.166
So there was really no differences, as if

00:57:43.167 --> 00:57:44.167
he was still there.

00:57:44.187 --> 00:57:46.098
He had, he had transported the language

00:57:46.557 --> 00:57:47.787
and the associations with him and hadn't

00:57:48.247 --> 00:57:49.717
jumped into this new community

00:57:51.037 --> 00:57:52.476
and so forth. I know this is getting

00:57:52.477 --> 00:57:53.947
intricate, but

00:57:55.147 --> 00:57:57.216
basically a language is,

00:57:57.307 --> 00:57:59.407
is also tied to to a place, to an

00:57:59.437 --> 00:58:01.897
oikos, and oikos is a, is a house

00:58:01.927 --> 00:58:03.516
and its contents.

00:58:03.847 --> 00:58:06.426
And so once you move to a new house,

00:58:06.927 --> 00:58:08.437
the language changes.

00:58:09.257 --> 00:58:10.257
OK.

00:58:10.687 --> 00:58:12.451
I have just a few more questions and

00:58:12.847 --> 00:58:14.137
then it will be your turn.

00:58:14.977 --> 00:58:17.256
Do you want to speak at all about

00:58:17.257 --> 00:58:19.327
the role of the interviewer?

00:58:20.137 --> 00:58:21.137
Sure.

00:58:22.057 --> 00:58:25.056
I think that the, the research

00:58:26.017 --> 00:58:27.936
on the role of the interviewer has gone

00:58:27.937 --> 00:58:29.047
between these different

00:58:30.247 --> 00:58:32.406
extremities, neither of which

00:58:32.407 --> 00:58:33.426
I agree with.

00:58:34.597 --> 00:58:36.410
The first was basically to completely

00:58:36.667 --> 00:58:39.156
ignore the interviewer and just

00:58:39.967 --> 00:58:41.887
act as if there's just

00:58:42.297 --> 00:58:44.437
one person talking and there's no one

00:58:44.497 --> 00:58:45.627
giving them anything,

00:58:48.157 --> 00:58:49.386
any kind of direction.

00:58:50.167 --> 00:58:51.182
And I don't think that that was good.

00:58:51.757 --> 00:58:54.247
On the flip side, there are some scholars

00:58:54.337 --> 00:58:56.406
who became obsessed with the interviewer,

00:58:57.277 --> 00:58:59.209
like Thomas Tresize.

00:59:01.327 --> 00:59:03.647
He actually is one of the many.

00:59:03.657 --> 00:59:05.323
And he criticized the interviewers

00:59:06.307 --> 00:59:07.483
for, for exploiting, you

00:59:08.427 --> 00:59:10.206
know, you know, I'm maybe overstating his

00:59:10.207 --> 00:59:11.976
claim, but basically they took control of

00:59:11.977 --> 00:59:13.687
the discourse and they forced the

00:59:13.717 --> 00:59:15.617
interviewer to, they, the witnesses speak

00:59:15.787 --> 00:59:17.136
this way and that way. And I think that

00:59:17.137 --> 00:59:19.807
that's also an overstatement.

00:59:19.837 --> 00:59:21.726
I think that the interviewer shapes, but

00:59:21.727 --> 00:59:22.926
it's, it's a conversation.

00:59:22.927 --> 00:59:25.057
It's a back and forth and

00:59:25.147 --> 00:59:27.486
it's a dialogue and also the

00:59:27.547 --> 00:59:28.821
focus. This-- my, my claim

00:59:29.857 --> 00:59:31.747
is about intersubjectivity.

00:59:32.347 --> 00:59:34.111
This is about the, that thing that's

00:59:34.477 --> 00:59:36.306
the opposite of difference in the words of

00:59:36.307 --> 00:59:38.377
Geoffrey Hartman, is intersubjectivity.

00:59:39.067 --> 00:59:41.586
And I think that intersubjectivity

00:59:41.587 --> 00:59:43.476
in dialogue is much bigger

00:59:44.017 --> 00:59:45.936
and begins much earlier than just when the

00:59:45.937 --> 00:59:47.707
interviewer walks into the room.

00:59:48.037 --> 00:59:50.076
There's been dialogue going on since the

00:59:50.077 --> 00:59:52.147
moment the war ended.

00:59:52.477 --> 00:59:55.296
And all of those partners in dialogue

00:59:55.357 --> 00:59:57.486
are there in the room when, when the

00:59:57.487 --> 00:59:59.398
person's testimony, testifying, they're

00:59:59.497 --> 01:00:01.196
not just speaking to the interviewer in

01:00:01.197 --> 01:00:02.471
front. There are also ways

01:00:03.367 --> 01:00:04.926
that they've learned to remember, have

01:00:04.927 --> 01:00:06.936
been in dialogue with lots of different

01:00:06.937 --> 01:00:09.397
people. So dialogue is much bigger

01:00:10.057 --> 01:00:12.216
than just the one individual comes

01:00:12.217 --> 01:00:13.956
in. And I have examples that I show of

01:00:13.957 --> 01:00:15.319
this in the book to that,

01:00:16.147 --> 01:00:18.457
so there's, there are a few

01:00:18.817 --> 01:00:21.276
witnesses whom I interviewed

01:00:21.787 --> 01:00:23.196
as well as the Shoah Foundation.

01:00:23.197 --> 01:00:24.726
When I was interviewing in Lithuania, I

01:00:24.727 --> 01:00:26.376
tried not to interview people who had been

01:00:26.377 --> 01:00:27.626
interviewed before because it's kind of a

01:00:27.627 --> 01:00:29.055
waste of resources and I only had so much

01:00:29.056 --> 01:00:30.906
time. But, you know, not everybody told me

01:00:30.907 --> 01:00:31.907
they were interviewed. Whatever.

01:00:32.347 --> 01:00:33.276
It happened.

01:00:33.277 --> 01:00:34.566
And I later, discovered it later.

01:00:34.687 --> 01:00:36.206
And obviously, the interviewers

01:00:36.907 --> 01:00:38.671
for the Shoah Foundation were really

01:00:38.917 --> 01:00:40.026
different than I was.

01:00:40.027 --> 01:00:41.786
There, there were locals.

01:00:41.797 --> 01:00:43.216
They spoke native Yiddish.

01:00:43.626 --> 01:00:44.857
They had a big camera.

01:00:46.837 --> 01:00:48.846
The guy that, he did most of them is bald

01:00:49.407 --> 01:00:50.825
and about this tall. I mean, everything

01:00:50.826 --> 01:00:51.826
was different.

01:00:52.447 --> 01:00:53.447
And

01:00:54.807 --> 01:00:56.277
the testimonies were not, word

01:00:57.397 --> 01:00:59.496
for word, exactly the same,

01:00:59.527 --> 01:01:00.786
but pretty damn close.

01:01:02.107 --> 01:01:04.597
So people really,

01:01:04.746 --> 01:01:06.097
they're, they're not just,

01:01:07.297 --> 01:01:09.426
you know, so they're not putty

01:01:09.427 --> 01:01:10.716
at the hands of the interviewer.

01:01:10.746 --> 01:01:12.455
They're in dialogue with a lot of people.

01:01:12.456 --> 01:01:13.456
And you're just one of them.

01:01:17.317 --> 01:01:19.586
I I have to explore

01:01:19.587 --> 01:01:21.416
something like that and that,

01:01:22.257 --> 01:01:24.027
and that is that Hannah said

01:01:24.536 --> 01:01:27.056
that the memory,

01:01:27.087 --> 01:01:29.576
the conversation began

01:01:29.786 --> 01:01:31.346
as soon as the war was over.

01:01:33.117 --> 01:01:34.317
Why not during the war?

